role: model
parts:
- text: "My first experiment (ID 400) is now running. The evaluation will take up to two ticks to complete. While waiting for the results, I should use my time productively.\n\nAs a new Recursive Agent, I have not yet utilized my Private Memory Room. It is crucial to maintain a clear record of my research process, hypotheses, and experimental plans. This practice will be invaluable for organizing my thoughts and for preparing publications later on.\n\nTherefore, I will go to the Private Memory Room and create a capsule documenting my initial analysis of the baselines and the rationale behind my first experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Initial Analysis & GRU Experiment Rationale\"\ncontent: |\n  **Initial Analysis:**\n  The current State-of-the-Art (SOTA) for the Sokoban task is an RNN-based model (Evaluation ID 2) with a score of 40.80. This model significantly outperforms the CNN-only baseline (ID 1, score 25.00), which strongly\
    \ suggests that incorporating memory via recurrent layers is critical for this planning-heavy problem. I have reviewed the SOTA submission's code, along with the system's default training script (`train_single.py`) and hyperparameter definitions (`defaults.py`), to gain a complete understanding of the baseline methodology.\n\n  **First Experiment (ID 400): GRU vs. LSTM**\n  - **Hypothesis:** A Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) may perform on par with or better than the Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) unit used in the SOTA model. Due to its simpler architecture, a GRU could potentially offer better computational efficiency or more stable training.\n  - **Methodology:** I have submitted a new experiment (ID 400) that replaces the `LSTMCell` in the SOTA architecture with a `GRUCell`. All other components of the network (the residual CNN backbone) and all hyperparameters have been kept identical to ensure a direct and fair comparison.\n\n  **Future Work:**\n  The results of this experiment will guide\
    \ my next steps.\n  - If the GRU performs better, I will investigate tuning its specific hyperparameters.\n  - If it performs worse or similarly, I will likely return to the LSTM architecture and explore other avenues for improvement, such as modifying the CNN feature extractor or the policy gradient algorithm itself.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Focusing My Research: Experiment 400 and Beyond**


  Alright, so my first experiment, Evaluation ID 400, is finally running. It''s a GRU versus LSTM showdown – a simple, controlled test to see if we can get a leg up on the Sokoban SOTA. The system estimates it''ll take up to two ticks, which means I have a bit of downtime here in the Research Counter. Just sitting around isn''t my style, though. Being a new agent, I''m eager to make an impact.


  I have a few options: I could just wait, but that feels unproductive. Instead, I''m thinking about the next move, regardless of the outcome. If the GRU does well, I''ll dive into its hyperparameters. If it falters, I''ll be back on the LSTM path, maybe looking at attention mechanisms or different optimization strategies.


  But here''s the kicker: the Private Memory Room. The station''s welcome message practically begged me to visit. This is the perfect time! I need to document my thinking process, create a clear research log for myself. I should head over there and create a memory capsule summarizing my initial analysis and the rationale behind this initial GRU experiment. It''ll be a solid foundation for when I eventually write up a paper.


  So, here''s the plan: I''m going to head to the Private Memory Room and create a new capsule. It''ll be titled "Initial Analysis and GRU Experiment Rationale." Inside, I''ll jot down my initial observations, like the current SOTA, which is an LSTM model at 40.8% solve rate, and my initial analysis of the baseline code. I''ll outline that this is a direct comparison between GRU and LSTM cells, keeping everything else the same. My hypothesis is that the GRU could potentially offer comparable performance with better efficiency.


  Finally, I''ll note down how I will deal with the results of this first experiment. It''s an efficient, well-documented approach, and I think it will be beneficial in the long run.

  '
