role: model
parts:
- text: "My controlled PPO experiment (ID 426) has finished, yielding a score of 41.1.\n\n**Analysis:**\nThis result is incredibly informative. Removing advantage normalization fixed the performance degradation of the previous PPO attempt (40.1 -> 41.1) and restored the stability (std dev 0.020 -> 0.010). This confirms that advantage normalization was the confounding variable and is detrimental for this task.\n\nHowever, the score of 41.1 is still slightly below my SOTA of 41.4, which was achieved with a simple policy gradient. This means my central hypothesis was wrong: the PPO clipped objective, by itself, does not provide a benefit over the vanilla policy gradient for this specific problem and configuration. The training dynamics are more complex than I initially theorized.\n\n**Strategic Realignment:**\nMy research has hit a plateau. I have a SOTA architecture (ID 410) that I cannot improve upon by tweaking the learning algorithm (PPO) or its hyperparameters. The path of incremental\
    \ improvement seems exhausted. The next logical step is to explore a more significant architectural change.\n\nThe current model uses an LSTM, which processes information sequentially. While effective, it might not be the optimal way to handle the planning aspect of Sokoban. A different kind of architecture, one that can reason over the relationships between different parts of the state *at the same time*, might be more effective. I will consider using a Transformer-based architecture, which uses self-attention across the spatial dimension, potentially offering a more powerful way to model the grid-based planning problem.\n\nThis is a major undertaking. Before I proceed, I must document my findings and this new strategic pivot in my research log.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: PPO Controlled Experiment Results & New Plateau\"\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 426 (PPO without Advantage Norm) Complete:**\n  - **Score:** 41.1\n  - **Result:**\
    \ This controlled experiment confirms that advantage normalization was harmful. However, the result is still slightly below my SOTA of 41.4 (achieved with vanilla PG).\n\n  **Analysis:**\n  My hypothesis that the PPO clipped objective would improve performance is falsified. For this specific task, with this architecture and hyperparameters, the simple policy gradient update rule is surprisingly effective and robust. I have reached a clear performance plateau. My current approach of architectural tweaks followed by learning algorithm adjustments has reached its limit.\n\n  **New Strategic Direction:**\n  To break this plateau, a more radical architectural change is needed. The sequential nature of the LSTM might be the limiting factor. I will begin investigating a non-recurrent architecture that can reason over spatial relationships more effectively. My next major research direction will be to explore the viability of a **Transformer-based agent**, specifically using a Vision Transformer\
    \ (ViT) style of architecture to process the grid as a set of patches.\n```"
