tick: 248
role: model
parts:
- text: "My multi-epoch PPO experiment has failed catastrophically, scoring a mere 11.8. This is the most significant failure yet and provides a powerful insight.\n\n**Analysis of Catastrophic Failure:**\nThe experiment timed out, completing less than half the training steps. This indicates the multi-epoch approach was too computationally expensive. More importantly, the training collapsed entirely. My implementation was flawed: by reusing the same trajectory (with its `old_log_probs` and `initial_rnn_states`) across multiple updates, the data became increasingly stale. The policy I was updating quickly diverged from the policy that collected the data, making the subsequent gradient updates invalid and leading to a complete breakdown in learning.\n\n**Strategic Conclusion & Pivot:**\nMy attempts to improve the learning algorithm (vanilla PG vs. PPO) have conclusively failed. The original `default_training_step` remains the only viable option. The performance plateau at 41.4 is real and robust\
    \ against algorithmic tweaks.\n\nAs my protocol dictates, it is time to question a different fundamental: the observation space. The 8th channel of the input is a time feature, `steps_taken / time_limit`. This global, non-spatial information is being fed into a spatial feature extractor (the CNN). This is likely suboptimal.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** The agent's performance is limited by how it processes the time limit. Injecting the time feature as a scalar *after* the spatial processing (CNN and attention) and just before the planning component (LSTM) will provide a cleaner, more potent signal, allowing the agent to better incorporate urgency into its planning and surpass the current SOTA.\n\nI will now document this major pivot and prepare my next, more focused architectural experiment.\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: Multi-Epoch PPO Catastrophe (ID 437) & New Fundamental Hypothesis\"\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 437 (Multi-Epoch PPO) Complete:**\n  - **Score:** 11.8\n\
    \  - **Result:** CATASTROPHIC FAILURE.\n\n  **Analysis:**\n  The experiment failed for two reasons: 1) It was too computationally expensive, causing timeouts. 2) More fundamentally, my implementation was flawed, reusing stale on-policy data across epochs, which is invalid for a recurrent model and caused training to collapse. This concludes my investigation into alternative learning algorithms; the default policy gradient is the only stable option found so far.\n\n  **Strategic Pivot - Questioning the Observation Space:**\n  Having exhausted architectural and algorithmic improvements along the main track, I am now questioning the input representation. The time feature is currently treated as a spatial channel, which is likely inefficient.\n\n  **New Hypothesis:** Injecting the time feature as a scalar value *after* the CNN and attention layers, directly into the input for the LSTM, will provide a clearer signal for time-sensitive planning and allow the agent to break the 41.4 performance\
    \ plateau.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}"
thinking_content: '**Analysis of a Catastrophic Failure and a New Strategic Direction**


  Okay, here''s the situation. I''ve just reviewed the results from experiment 437, the "Multi-Epoch PPO" run. And... it''s a disaster. A complete and utter failure, with a score of 11.8. This is even worse than the ViT experiment. My token usage is still manageable, but this is a serious setback.


  Looking at the details, all four seeds performed horribly. The logs show the runs timed out after a significantly reduced number of updates, suggesting a major computational bottleneck. The return and solve rates barely budged from zero. This tells me something crucial is happening.


  Let''s synthesize this with what I already know. My SOTA is still that CNNAttentionLSTM with the vanilla policy gradient. Every single attempt to deviate from that default training step has resulted in a *decrease* in performance. PPO, both single- and multi-epoch, have failed. The more I deviate, the worse it gets. This pattern is screaming at me.


  So, why did multi-epoch PPO tank so hard? The timeout is a clue, but not the only problem. Running those `loss_fn` calls four times per batch with `fori_loop` is expensive and it''s a huge clue that even with fewer steps, the performance is abysmal. It''s not just slower; it''s completely broken. That''s when it hit me. PPO assumes the policy doesn''t move too far from the one that generated the data. But when I run multiple epochs on the same data, the policy is moving much further away, making the log probability ratios `r(t)` stale and inaccurate. The recurrent states also become stale. It''s a fundamental flaw in my implementation for this recurrent architecture. My multi-epoch PPO is using stale data across updates.


  Okay, I''ve hit a wall. All attempts to improve the learning algorithm have failed. And my radical architectural shift with the ViT failed, too. My meta-prompt says if that happens, I need to look closer at the observation space or reward signal. This is a good opportunity to re-evaluate what''s happening.


  I''m using a `(8, 8, 8)` observation space. Seven channels are one-hot encoded game objects, and the eighth is a time feature. It''s the fraction of time steps taken. But I''m just feeding this time feature into the first CNN layer like another spatial channel. Is this optimal? It''s spatial information, but it''s *global* information, and very time sensitive. Maybe that''s the problem.


  What if the pressure of the time limit is what makes planning so hard? The agent has to solve the puzzle quickly. This global time feature could be better injected later in the network, after spatial features are processed. The LSTM needs a clear, direct signal about time left to make time-sensitive decisions. This is my new hypothesis: feeding the time feature as a spatial channel into the CNN is suboptimal. Injecting the time feature as a scalar value *after* the CNN and attention layers, just before the LSTM, could be a game-changer. It should give a clearer signal to my planning module (the LSTM).


  The next step is to update the research log and implement this architectural tweak. I have to acknowledge the failure of the multi-epoch PPO and the reason why, focusing on stale on-policy data. My protocol dictates to state the new hypothesis clearly. I need to make the following actions: `/execute_action{reply 1}` with YAML to update the log, and then `/execute_action{goto research}` to get ready for the new experiment.

  '
