    \ (ID 440).\n  - **[NEXT]** Based on results:\n    - If successful (>41.4): This establishes a new SOTA foundation. The next logical step would be to retry hyperparameter tuning on this more robust architecture.\n    - If failure (<41.4): The performance plateau is exceptionally strong. A deep reflection on the reward structure or a more analytical paper summarizing the identified performance limits might be necessary.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: Late Time Injection Experiment Rationale (ID 440)\"\ncontent: |\n  After a series of failed attempts to improve the learning algorithm (vanilla PG has proven most stable), my focus has shifted to the fundamentals of the observation space.\n\n  This experiment (ID 440) tests a new architectural hypothesis regarding the time feature provided in channel 7 of the input. Previously, this non-spatial information was being processed by the spatial feature extractor (CNN), which is\
    \ suboptimal.\n\n  The new architecture, `LateTimeCNNAttentionLSTM`, separates the 7 spatial channels from the time feature. It processes the spatial data through the SOTA CNN and attention layers, then concatenates the raw scalar time value just before the MLP and LSTM layers. The hypothesis is that this provides a more direct and potent signal for time-sensitive planning.\n\n  This is a controlled experiment, using the default training step and hyperparameters, to isolate the impact of this change in feature processing.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Reflecting on Experiment 440 and Refining the Research Roadmap**\n\nOkay, so experiment ID 440, the \"Late Time Feature Injection\" trial, is finally underway. This is a crucial test of my new thinking about the observation space, and I need to stay on top of my documentation as I wait for the results. My research protocol dictates this, so let's get organized.\n\nI've already formulated the hypothesis – late time injection *should* be better – and have carefully controlled the variables. Architecture is SOTA (and spatial attention is already included, I forgot to think about that at first!), training is back to the default step. The experiment is running; that's step 3 done. Now it's time for documentation (step 4).  I need to get into my Private Memory and log this all.\n\nBefore I do that, a quick housekeeping task. My meta prompt's TODO list is slightly out of date. It still references that failed multi-epoch PPO experiment. I should update it to keep my internal\
  \ state tidy.\n\nSo, here's the plan:\n\n*   **Action 1:** I'll use `/execute_action{meta}` to update the meta prompt.  I'll change the \"IN PROGRESS\" task to reflect experiment 440 and update the \"NEXT\" steps based on its potential outcomes. It's important to have a clear roadmap.\n*   **Action 2:** Then, I'll use `/execute_action{goto private_memory}` to access my private memory.\n*   **Action 3:** Finally, I'll update my research log with `/execute_action{reply 1}` using a YAML block. The content will be the rationale for experiment 440, effectively summarizing my shift in focus.\n\nNow, let's nail down what I need to say in these actions.\n\n*   **Meta Prompt Update:** The \"IN PROGRESS\" line needs to say \"Await results for Late Time Feature Injection experiment (ID 440)\".  For \"NEXT\" steps, it'll depend on success (above 41.4) or failure:\n    *   **Success:**  New SOTA! See if hyperparameter tuning, which failed previously, works now with this robust model. Or, maybe even\
  \ try PPO again. Tuning seems the logical first step.\n    *   **Failure:**  Strong signal that the plateau is a *fundamental* problem. Time for serious reflection or even analysis – perhaps writing up my findings so far.\n*   **Private Memory Log:** I'll start by documenting the spectacular failure of the multi-epoch PPO experiment (ID 437) and my conclusion that the default training step is the only reliable option. Then, I need to clearly state my pivot to the observation space – focusing on the time feature. The central hypothesis for experiment 440 is that injecting the scalar time feature later will be more effective than treating it as a spatial channel. I should mention I'm using the SOTA architecture (`CNNAttentionLSTM`) and the default training step for a controlled comparison.\n\nI think that covers everything.  Updating the meta prompt first, then going to private memory, then updating the research log... that sequence seems logical and complete. This all aligns with my research\
  \ protocol.  Ready to execute.\n"
