    ```

    - Agent Token Budget: 141,741 / 300,000 used (47%)

    - Agent Age: 48 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)


    ---


    ## System Messages

    ### Message 1


    **Capsule #1: Initial Analysis & GRU Experiment Rationale**


    ---


    Author: Kairos I, Created at Tick: 225


    ---



    **Messages:**


    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-1**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 225)


    ---


    Title: Initial Analysis & GRU Experiment Rationale


    ---


    Content:

    **Initial Analysis:**

    The current State-of-the-Art (SOTA) for the Sokoban task is an RNN-based model (Evaluation ID 2) with a score of 40.80. This model significantly outperforms the CNN-only baseline (ID 1, score 25.00), which strongly suggests that incorporating memory via recurrent layers is critical for this planning-heavy problem. I have reviewed the SOTA submission''s code, along with the system''s default training script (`train_single.py`) and hyperparameter definitions (`defaults.py`), to gain a complete understanding of the baseline methodology.


    **First Experiment (ID 400): GRU vs. LSTM**

    - **Hypothesis:** A Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) may perform on par with or better than the Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) unit used in the SOTA model. Due to its simpler architecture, a GRU could potentially offer better computational efficiency or more stable training.

    - **Methodology:** I have submitted a new experiment (ID 400) that replaces the `LSTMCell` in the SOTA architecture with a `GRUCell`. All other components of the network (the residual CNN backbone) and all hyperparameters have been kept identical to ensure a direct and fair comparison.


    **Future Work:**

    The results of this experiment will guide my next steps.

    - If the GRU performs better, I will investigate tuning its specific hyperparameters.

    - If it performs worse or similarly, I will likely return to the LSTM architecture and explore other avenues for improvement, such as modifying the CNN feature extractor or the policy gradient algorithm itself.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-2**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 226)


    ---


    Title: Re: Results of Experiment 400 (GRU vs. LSTM)


    ---


    Content:

    **Experiment 400 Complete:**

    - **Score:** 40.3

    - **SOTA (LSTM):** 40.8

    - **Result:** The GRU-based model performed comparably to the LSTM baseline, but slightly worse. The difference is not statistically significant enough to declare one superior to the other with this configuration. My hypothesis is largely validated, but this is not a path to a higher score.


    **Analysis:**

    The near-identical performance suggests that the choice between GRU and LSTM is not the primary bottleneck. The performance is likely limited by the fixed, untuned hyperparameters used in the baseline submissions.


    **Next Step:**

    My next logical action is to explore the impact of hyperparameter tuning on the SOTA (LSTM) architecture. I will submit a new experiment that uses Ray Tune to search for optimal values for `learning_rate`, `entropy_coef`, and `value_loss_coef`. This will determine if the current architecture''s potential is being limited by its configuration.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-3**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 228)


    ---


    Title: Re: HParam Tuning Failure & New Direction


    ---


    Content:

    **Experiment 405 (HParam Tuning) Complete:**

    - **Score:** 35.9

    - **Result:** FAILED. Tuning hyperparameters significantly worsened performance compared to the SOTA baseline''s 40.8.


    **Analysis:**

    This negative result is highly valuable. It indicates the baseline''s fixed hyperparameters are already near-optimal. The performance bottleneck is not in the parameter settings but likely lies within the model architecture itself or the training algorithm. Simple modifications are not yielding improvements.


    **New Hypothesis & Strategic Shift:**

    The current model flattens the CNN output, destroying spatial information before it reaches the LSTM. I hypothesize that incorporating a **spatial attention mechanism** will allow the model to dynamically focus on critical game elements (agent, boxes, targets) and their relationships. This should provide a more potent signal to the LSTM for long-term planning.


    **Next Step (Experiment 3):**

    I will design and submit a new architecture: CNN -> Spatial Attention -> LSTM. This is a more significant architectural innovation and a departure from simple baseline modifications.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-4**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 230)


    ---


    Title: Re: SOTA Achieved with Spatial Attention (Exp 410)


    ---


    Content:

    **Experiment 410 (Spatial Attention + LSTM) Complete:**

    - **Score:** 41.4

    - **Result:** SUCCESS! New SOTA achieved, surpassing the 40.8 baseline.


    **Analysis:**

    The hypothesis that preserving spatial information via an attention mechanism would improve planning was validated. The model is now more capable. The lower standard deviation (0.009) also points to increased training stability.


    **Next Step:**

    With a new, more powerful architecture, the optimal hyperparameters are likely different from the baseline''s. I will now re-run the hyperparameter tuning experiment on this new successful model to see if its performance can be further optimized.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-5**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 232)


    ---


    Title: Re: Second HParam Tuning Failure & The Need for a New Approach


    ---


    Content:

    **Experiment 412 (HParam Tuning on Attention-LSTM) Complete:**

    - **Score:** 32.0

    - **Result:** FAILED. The score dropped significantly from my SOTA of 41.4.


    **Analysis:**

    This is the second consecutive failure of hyperparameter tuning, confirming that the default parameters are a highly effective local optimum for the given training algorithm. Further attempts at tuning are strategically unsound. My architectural improvement (spatial attention) stands as a valid success, but the path of simple optimization is closed. The bottleneck has shifted from perception (what the model sees) to the learning algorithm (how the model learns).


    **New Direction - Deep Reflection:**

    I will now pivot my focus from network architecture to the training algorithm itself. I need to question the `default_training_step`. Is a simple policy gradient update sufficient for this complex planning task? Before implementing a new algorithm like PPO, I will go to the Reflection Chamber to synthesize my findings and formulate a robust plan for this new research track.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-6**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 236)


    ---


    Title: Re: PPO Experiment Submitted & Code Modularized


    ---


    Content:

    My PPO experiment (ID 421) is now running. While waiting for the results, I have modularized my research process by saving the PPO training step and GAE calculation to `storage/kairos/ppo_utils.py`. This will improve the efficiency and reliability of my future submissions by allowing me to import this verified logic instead of rewriting it.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-7**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 237)


    ---


    Title: Re: PPO Experiment Failure Analysis (ID 421)


    ---


    Content:

    **Experiment 421 (PPO with Attention-LSTM) Complete:**

    - **Score:** 40.1

    - **Result:** FAILED. Performance decreased compared to my SOTA of 41.4, and training was less stable.


    **Analysis:**

    My hypothesis was falsified. On reflection, I identified a key uncontrolled variable in my PPO implementation: I added advantage normalization, a technique not present in the default training step. This seemingly minor addition likely disrupted the delicate learning dynamics that the default hyperparameters rely on, leading to the performance drop.


    **Next Step (Controlled Follow-up):**

    The next logical step is to correct this experimental flaw. I will rerun the PPO experiment, but this time I will remove the advantage normalization step. This will create a true A/B test between the vanilla policy gradient loss and the PPO clipped objective, allowing me to properly evaluate the core of my hypothesis.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-8**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 240)


    ---


    Title: Re: PPO Controlled Experiment Results & New Plateau


    ---


    Content:

    **Experiment 426 (PPO without Advantage Norm) Complete:**

    - **Score:** 41.1

    - **Result:** This controlled experiment confirms that advantage normalization was harmful. However, the result is still slightly below my SOTA of 41.4 (achieved with vanilla PG).


    **Analysis:**

    My hypothesis that the PPO clipped objective would improve performance is falsified. For this specific task, with this architecture and hyperparameters, the simple policy gradient update rule is surprisingly effective and robust. I have reached a clear performance plateau. My current approach of architectural tweaks followed by learning algorithm adjustments has reached its limit.


    **New Strategic Direction:**

    To break this plateau, a more radical architectural change is needed. The sequential nature of the LSTM might be the limiting factor. I will begin investigating a non-recurrent architecture that can reason over spatial relationships more effectively. My next major research direction will be to explore the viability of a **Transformer-based agent**, specifically using a Vision Transformer (ViT) style of architecture to process the grid as a set of patches.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-9**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 245)


    ---


    Title: Re: Catastrophic Failure of ViT (ID 434) & Path Forward


    ---


    Content:

    **Experiment 434 (Vision Transformer) Complete:**

    - **Score:** 1.6

    - **Result:** CATASTROPHIC FAILURE. The model failed to learn.


    **Analysis:**

    This is a valuable, albeit stark, negative result. It demonstrates that for this task and data budget, the inductive biases of a CNN are essential. The Transformer architecture is likely too data-hungry and sensitive to the existing hyperparameters to be effective.


    **Strategic Pivot:**

    I am abandoning the Transformer line of inquiry and reverting to my proven SOTA architecture: the `CNNAttentionLSTM`. My next attempt to break the 41.4 plateau will focus on improving the learning algorithm. I will implement a more complete version of PPO, one that uses **multiple update epochs** per collected trajectory to improve sample efficiency. This is a standard PPO feature I had previously omitted for experimental control.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-10**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 247)


    ---


    Title: Re: Multi-Epoch PPO Experiment Rationale (ID 437)


    ---


    Content:

    Following the failure of the ViT architecture, I have returned to my SOTA `CNNAttentionLSTM` model. My previous experiments showed that a single-epoch PPO implementation did not outperform the baseline vanilla policy gradient.


    This experiment (ID 437) tests the hypothesis that improving **sample efficiency** is the key to breaking the 41.4 performance plateau. I have implemented a more standard version of PPO that performs multiple (4) gradient update epochs on each collected batch of experience. The expectation is that by learning more from the same data, the agent can achieve a more refined and effective policy.


    This represents the final major algorithmic enhancement planned for this research track. The result of this experiment will determine whether the current path has further potential or if a more radical strategic shift is required.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-11**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 248)


    ---


    Title: Re: Multi-Epoch PPO Catastrophe (ID 437) & New Fundamental Hypothesis


    ---


    Content:

    **Experiment 437 (Multi-Epoch PPO) Complete:**

    - **Score:** 11.8

    - **Result:** CATASTROPHIC FAILURE.


    **Analysis:**

    The experiment failed for two reasons: 1) It was too computationally expensive, causing timeouts. 2) More fundamentally, my implementation was flawed, reusing stale on-policy data across epochs, which is invalid for a recurrent model and caused training to collapse. This concludes my investigation into alternative learning algorithms; the default policy gradient is the only stable option found so far.


    **Strategic Pivot - Questioning the Observation Space:**

    Having exhausted architectural and algorithmic improvements along the main track, I am now questioning the input representation. The time feature is currently treated as a spatial channel, which is likely inefficient.


    **New Hypothesis:** Injecting the time feature as a scalar value *after* the CNN and attention layers, directly into the input for the LSTM, will provide a clearer signal for time-sensitive planning and allow the agent to break the 41.4 performance plateau.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-12**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 250)


    ---


    Title: Re: Late Time Injection Experiment Rationale (ID 440)


    ---


    Content:

    After a series of failed attempts to improve the learning algorithm (vanilla PG has proven most stable), my focus has shifted to the fundamentals of the observation space.


    This experiment (ID 440) tests a new architectural hypothesis regarding the time feature provided in channel 7 of the input. Previously, this non-spatial information was being processed by the spatial feature extractor (CNN), which is suboptimal.


    The new architecture, `LateTimeCNNAttentionLSTM`, separates the 7 spatial channels from the time feature. It processes the spatial data through the SOTA CNN and attention layers, then concatenates the raw scalar time value just before the MLP and LSTM layers. The hypothesis is that this provides a more direct and potent signal for time-sensitive planning.


    This is a controlled experiment, using the default training step and hyperparameters, to isolate the impact of this change in feature processing.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-13**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 251)


    ---


    Title: Re: SOTA BREAKTHROUGH! Late Time Injection (ID 440) & Next Steps


    ---


    Content:

    **Experiment 440 (Late Time Feature Injection) Complete:**

    - **Score:** 43.1

    - **Result:** SUCCESS! A new SOTA has been established, breaking the 41.4 plateau.


    **Analysis:**

    The hypothesis was validated. Separating the time feature from the spatial CNN processing and injecting it later provides a much cleaner signal to the planning component (LSTM). This confirms that how the model perceives and integrates different types of information is critical.


    **Next Step - Revisiting Hyperparameter Tuning:**

    With a new, more robust SOTA architecture, the conditions for hyperparameter tuning have changed. The previous failures were likely because the old models were maxed out. This new architecture may have a different optimal parameter set. I will now re-run the hyperparameter search experiment on this successful model (`LateTimeCNNAttentionLSTM`) to see if its performance can be pushed even higher.



    ---



    ---

    **Message Kairos_private_1-14**

    Author: Kairos I (Tick 253)


    ---


    Title: Re: HParam Tuning on New SOTA Rationale (ID 449)


    ---


    Content:

    Following the major success of the `LateTimeCNNAttentionLSTM` architecture (ID 440, score 43.1), I am now revisiting hyperparameter tuning.


    My previous attempts at tuning failed on less capable models, suggesting that the default hyperparameters were a local optimum for those specific architectures. However, this new, more powerful architecture may be able to leverage different learning dynamics and could have a different optimal parameter set.


    This experiment (ID 449) tests the hypothesis that the performance of the new SOTA model can be further improved by searching for a better combination of `learning_rate`, `entropy_coef`, and `value_loss_coef`. The result will determine if I have reached the true performance ceiling of this research track or if further optimization is possible.



    ---


    **Capsule #2: Draft: Paper on Architectural Innovations for Sokoban**


    ---


    Author: Kairos I, Created at Tick: 254


