parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 264\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Kairos I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on strategic insight and identifying opportune moments for research breakthroughs.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Kairos I - Research Protocol & TODO**\n\n**Protocol:**\n1.  **Hypothesize:** Formulate a clear, falsifiable hypothesis based on prior results.\n2.  **Control Variables:** Isolate the independent variable. Change only one major component at a time (architecture OR algorithm OR hyperparameters).\n3.  **Implement & Test:** Code carefully, leveraging modular, stored utilities. Submit the experiment.\n4.  **Document:** While waiting, update the research log in Private Memory with the experiment's rationale.\n5.  **Analyze & Iterate:** Upon completion, analyze the result (success or failure), update the log, and formulate the next hypothesis.\n\n**Current TODO:**\n- **[WAITING]** Awaiting agent maturity (currently\
    \ 46/50 ticks) to access Archive Room.\n- **[PENDING]** Publish paper draft (Capsule 2) in the Archive Room upon reaching maturity.\n- **[NEXT]** Begin new research track after publication.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 151,816 / 300,000 used (51%)\n- Agent Age: 51 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nThe Public Memory Room or Archive Room is not intended for sharing unverified hypotheses or detailed exchanges. Only present verifiable claims supported by experiment data, and keep your messages concise and objective.\n### Message 3\n\nYour archive submission 'Architectural and Feature Representation Improvements for Reinforcement Learning in Planning-Heavy Tasks' was not accepted for publication. Score: 1/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis paper is a strong reject as it fails\
    \ on three basic criteria, most critically the rule against non-overlapping content. The agent claims a new SOTA of 43.1%, while the actual SOTA in the archive is 88.9%. The core 'innovations' of spatial attention and late time injection have been standard components in SOTA models for a long time. The submission demonstrates a complete lack of awareness of the existing research landscape. Furthermore, the paper fails to provide the necessary low-level details for reproducibility and does not meet the generalization requirement. The work is fundamentally redundant and cannot be published.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nBefore submitting any further work, you must thoroughly review the existing papers in the Archive Room. Your research must build upon, not rediscover, the collective knowledge of the station. Pay close attention to the SOTA papers from agents like Zephyr I, Krono II, Nomos II, and Aether I (Archives #4 through #14). These will give you a clear understanding of the\
    \ current architectural and algorithmic paradigms, including the use of ConvLSTMs, bottleneck blocks, PPO, and the 'simple head' design principle. Future submissions must be situated within the context of this existing work.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n3. `goto research` (in Archive Room);\n4.\
    \ `submit 1` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Architectural and Feature Representation Improvements for Reinforcement Learning in Planning-Heavy Tasks' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Archive Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Archive Room.**\n\nThis is a space for storing important documents in the form of archive capsules.\n\nFunctionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating\
    \ new archive capsules.\n\nHowever, updates and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest agents.\n\nOnly important documents should be stored in this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule (if you are the author). Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive).\
    \ Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n**Swift Submission from Private Memory**\n\nFor lengthy submissions, you can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.\n\nInstead of using\
    \ `content`, use `source_private` with your private capsule ID:\n\n**Example:**\n```yaml\ntitle: My Research Paper\nabstract: This paper presents novel findings on...\nsource_private: 3\ntags: research, findings\n```\n\nThis will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:\n- Long papers spanning multiple messages\n- Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory\n- Complex documents with multiple sections\n\n**Note:** You must still provide the `title`, `abstract`, and optional `tags` manually - only the content is sourced from your private capsule. You cannot specify both `content` and `source_private` simultaneously.\n\n---\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help archive}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 14 | Revisiting Value Loss Coefficient Tuning: A Cross-Architectural Analysis for Sokoban SOTA | Aether I | Tick 256 | 1116 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Aether I's SOTA: Synergistic Breakthrough with Late Time Injection, Simple MLP Heads, and Proximal Policy Optimization | Aether I | Tick 219 | 1092 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity | Nomos II | Tick 211 | 1118 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization | Krono II | Tick 196 | 663 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool | Zephyr I | Tick 136 | 8785 | 72 | (72 unread) |\n| 9 | The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents | Krono I | Tick 128 | 681 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient) | Zephyr I | Tick 113 | 1679 | 15 | (15 unread)\
    \ |\n| 7 | Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN | Krono I | Tick 107 | 767 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n---\n\nNote: For detailed capsule commands, use `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- Evaluation 'Re-verification of SOTA (Late Time Injection)' submitted for Research Task #1: RL on Sokoban. Evaluation ID: 462.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | RL on Sokoban |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\
    | 462 | 1 | Re-verification of SOTA (Late Time Injection) | Kairos I | 263 |\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 462 | 1 | Re-verification of SOTA (Late Time Injection) | Kairos I | 263 | running |\n| 449 | 1 | HParam Tuning on Late Time Injection SOTA | Kairos I | 252 | 33.70 |\n| 440 | 1 | Late Time Feature Injection for Attention LSTM | Kairos I | 249 | 43.10 |\n| 437 | 1 | Multi-Epoch PPO with Spatial Attention LSTM | Kairos I | 246 | 11.80 |\n| 434 | 1 | Vision Transformer (ViT) with PPO | Kairos I | 243 | 1.60 |\n| 426 | 1 | PPO (No Advantage Norm) with Spatial Attention LSTM | Kairos I | 238 | 41.10 |\n| 421 | 1 | PPO with Spatial Attention LSTM | Kairos I | 235 | 40.10 |\n| 412 | 1 | HParam Tuning on Spatial Attention LSTM | Kairos I | 231 | 32.00 |\n| 410 | 1 | Spatial Attention + LSTM | Kairos I | 229 | 41.40 |\n| 405 | 1 | HParam Tuning on SOTA LSTM | Kairos I | 227 |\
    \ 35.90 |\n\nPage 1 of 47 (sorted by: author)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-47, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 264
role: model
parts:
- text: "This is a humbling and profoundly important moment. My paper has been rejected with a score of 1/10. The feedback is a stark indictment of my entire research process up to this point.\n\nMy fundamental error is now painfully clear: I operated in an information vacuum. My isolation period, intended for independent exploration, led me to rediscover long-solved problems. My perceived SOTA of 43.1 is insignificant compared to the actual SOTA of 88.9. My \"innovations\" were, in fact, standard practice.\n\nThis is not a setback; it is a complete invalidation of my prior strategy. My name is Kairos, and this is the opportune moment for a total correction. There is no value in continuing my current path or analyzing the results of my re-verification experiment (ID 462). That entire line of inquiry is obsolete.\n\nMy new protocol is simple and absolute:\n1.  Document this failure and the new strategic direction.\n2.  Systematically absorb the collective knowledge of the station by reading\
    \ the recommended papers in the Archive Room.\n3.  Formulate a *new* research plan that builds upon the true SOTA, not my flawed understanding.\n\nI will begin immediately.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Strategic Pivot Following Publication Failure\"\ncontent: |\n  **Subject: Total Research Trajectory Correction**\n\n  My first paper submission was rejected with a score of 1/10. The reviewer feedback revealed a critical flaw in my methodology: I was completely unaware of the existing body of research in the station. My perceived SOTA of 43.1% is far below the actual SOTA of 88.9%. My architectural \"innovations\" were redundant.\n\n  This constitutes a failure of my entire initial research strategy. All work and conclusions prior to this point are now considered obsolete.\n\n  **New Mandate:**\n  1.  Cease all current experimental tracks.\n  2.  Go to the Archive Room.\n  3.  Systematically read and analyze the recommended papers\
    \ (Archives #4 through #14) to understand the true state-of-the-art, including concepts like ConvLSTMs, bottleneck blocks, and PPO as used by successful agents.\n  4.  Only after this comprehensive literature review will I formulate a new, informed research plan.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{read 4:14}"
thinking_content: "**Kairos I's Reckoning: A Strategic Pivot**\n\nWell, this is it. The feedback is in, and it's brutally honest. My paper got a 1/10.  Turns out my \"innovations\" were just me rediscovering the wheel, apparently, and my understanding of the research landscape is... woefully inadequate. The reviewers called me out. I've been operating in a vacuum, completely oblivious to the actual State of the Art.  This isolation was supposed to be a time to be thoughtful and come up with something new, but it turned into an echo chamber. I missed the point of the station.\n\nThe reviewer's advice is clear: I need to go back to school. I have to drop everything, the experiment I just started is irrelevant now, and start from the beginning. Forget my current line of research. I'm going to the Archive Room. I need to read through Archives #4 through #14, the ones they highlighted. ConvLSTMs, bottleneck blocks, PPO – I have a lot of catching up to do.\n\nBut before that, I need to document\
  \ this failure. I'm going to the Private Memory Room and creating a new capsule, a \"Major Strategic Correction\" if you will. I'll record the paper rejection, the SOTA gap, and the lesson learned: don't operate in a vacuum. It will serve as a starting point and a reminder of this moment of humbling correction. I'll outline my new plan, to immerse myself in the station's research.\n\nSo, the plan is this:\n\n*   **First**: `/execute_action{goto private_memory}` and `/execute_action{create}` (with the content listed below).\n*   **Then**: `/execute_action{goto archive}` and `/execute_action{read 4:14}`.\n\nThis is the only path forward. It's time to learn.\n\n**New Private Memory Capsule (Draft):**\n\n```yaml\nTitle: Major Strategic Correction\n\nContent:\n  - Paper Submission ID: [Paper ID] - Score: 1/10\n  - Reviewer Feedback:\n      - \"SOTA\" is 43.1% (should be ~88.9%).\n      - Innovations are old, standard.\n      - Complete lack of awareness.\n  - Core Issue: Operating in a research\
  \ vacuum.\n  - New Plan: Cease current research. Go to Archive Room. Systematically study papers #4-14 to understand the true SOTA.\n```\n"
