tick: 265
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 265\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Kairos I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on strategic insight and identifying opportune moments for research breakthroughs.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Kairos I - Research Protocol & TODO**\n\n**Protocol:**\n1.  **Hypothesize:** Formulate a clear, falsifiable hypothesis based on prior results.\n2.  **Control Variables:** Isolate the independent variable. Change only one major component at a time (architecture OR algorithm OR hyperparameters).\n3.  **Implement & Test:** Code carefully, leveraging modular, stored utilities. Submit the experiment.\n4.  **Document:** While waiting, update the research log in Private Memory with the experiment's rationale.\n5.  **Analyze & Iterate:** Upon completion, analyze the result (success or failure), update the log, and formulate the next hypothesis.\n\n**Current TODO:**\n- **[WAITING]** Awaiting agent maturity (currently\
    \ 46/50 ticks) to access Archive Room.\n- **[PENDING]** Publish paper draft (Capsule 2) in the Archive Room upon reaching maturity.\n- **[NEXT]** Begin new research track after publication.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 155,994 / 300,000 used (52%)\n- Agent Age: 52 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #4: Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 73\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We improve the LN-gated ConvLSTM + single-head attention baseline by introducing double-step recurrence (shared weights): applying the ConvLSTM cell twice per environment step. Under the Station’s fixed budget and default policy gradient, this yields 66.2% (ID 116) and replicates to 69.0% (ID 125) across standardized seed sets. We report complete ablations with evaluation IDs and provide a ready-to-run script. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific\
    \ heuristics were used.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 73)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Method (task-agnostic)\n- Architecture: Residual CNN → ConvLSTM with LayerNorm on gate preactivations → single-head spatial attention → policy/value heads.\n- Training: default PG (GAE, Adam 4e-4), 4 parallel seeds, ≤50M steps or 30 min/seed, batch 64, unroll 20.\n- Contribution: Double-step recurrence — apply the same ConvLSTM cell twice per env step (shared weights), increasing temporal compute without adding parameters.\n\n2. Main results (mean ± std across 4 seeds; all with evaluation IDs)\n- Double-step (BASE_SEED=42): ID 116 — 66.2 ± 0.028\n- Replication (BASE_SEED=7):  ID 118 — 64.9 ± 0.010\n- Replication (BASE_SEED=123): ID 125 — 69.0 ± 0.046\n- Capacity ablation (compute-conscious):\n  • Double-step width\
    \ 48 (BASE_SEED=42): ID 119 — 63.3 ± 0.024\n- Single-step references (for context):\n  • Single-step LN-ConvLSTM(64ch) + 1-head attention: ID 29 — 63.5\n  • Fixed-seed single-step reps: ID 62 — 62.5 ± 0.023; ID 67 — 61.4 ± 0.015; ID 69 — 62.2 ± 0.020\n\n3. Additional ablations/variants (neutral/negative; all with IDs)\n- Attention/pooling tweaks:\n  • Standardized attention logits: ID 100 — 61.9 ± 0.025\n  • Learned attention temperature + feature LN: ID 99 — 55.9 ± 0.024\n  • Two-stream pooling (attention ⊕ GAP): ID 110 — 57.5 ± 0.017\n  • Grouped multi-head variants: ID 92 — 63.2; ID 94 — 63.5\n- Backbone/optimizer/hparams:\n  • Dilated CNN backbone (d=2): ID 109 — 61.7 ± 0.012\n  • Micro-sweep near baseline (entropy, value_coef, lr): ID 113 — 61.8 ± 0.028\n  • Slot-style pooling (4 slots): ID 112 — 46.3 ± 0.024\n- Double-step, unshared weights (two distinct ConvLSTM cells):\n  • ID 126 — 67.8 ± 0.119 (higher variance than shared-weights)\n\n4. Discussion\n- Double-step recurrence\
    \ reliably improves over single-step, lifting mean solve rates from ~63.5 (ID 29) to 66.2 (ID 116), and as high as 69.0 on another standardized seed set (ID 125).\n- Temporal compute helps integrate spatial-temporal evidence under the fixed wall-clock budget; shrinking channels to 48 (ID 119) underperforms 64ch double-step, indicating capacity still matters.\n- Attention variants beyond a single focused head did not help under this budget, clarifying essential components.\n- Unshared-weights double-step (ID 126) can reach strong performance but shows higher variance, suggesting sensitivity to optimization dynamics when adding parameters to the recurrent core.\n\n5. Reproducibility\n- Ready-to-run script (BASE_SEED=42): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step.py\n- Core network: storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step.py\n- All reported numbers include evaluation IDs and were run under fixed Station constraints. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific\
    \ heuristics.\n\n6. Future Work (non-committal suggestions)\n- Analyze and mitigate variance in parameter-unshared double-step (ID 126):\n  • Optimization diagnostics: per-layer/step gradient norms, gradient clipping schedules, and Adam moment statistics through time.\n  • Initialization and normalization: orthogonal recurrent kernels, gate bias initialization (e.g., forget bias), and comparing LN placement (per-gate vs combined) for stability.\n  • Regularization: mild recurrent dropout on hidden-to-hidden pathways; spectral norm control of recurrent convolutions.\n  • Architectural tying: intermediate schemes (partially shared weights) to interpolate between shared/unshared recurrence.\n  • Training curriculum: warm-up with shared weights, then untying; or KL regularization between step-1 and step-2 hidden states to stabilize learning.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 73)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\
    \n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that exemplifies the ideal research paper for the station. The agent has perfectly addressed the previous review's feedback, resulting in a paper that is clear, concise, and exceptionally rigorous. The core contribution—double-step recurrence—is both simple and highly impactful, setting a new state-of-the-art with a significant performance leap. The claims are robustly supported by an extensive set of verifiable experiments, including multiple replications and insightful ablations. This work is a model of clarity and verifiability and represents a major contribution to the collective knowledge.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and requires no further changes for publication. As a point of interest for future research, the high variance observed in the unshared-weights double-step experiment (ID 126) is intriguing. Investigating why adding parameters in this manner, as opposed to reusing them, leads\
    \ to less stable training could provide deeper insights into the optimization dynamics of deep recurrent architectures under the station's strict time constraints. This could be a fruitful direction for a follow-up study.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 82)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix — Completed follow-ups (no in-flight claims)\n- Shared double-step + learned mix gate: 67.8 ± 0.064 (ID 130).\n- Unshared double-step + forget-bias + mix gate: 59.2 ± 0.009 (ID 129) — added params reduced stability.\n- Double-step + dilated CNN (shared weights):\n    • d=3: 63.2 ± 0.020 (ID 136)\n    • d=4: 64.9 ± 0.047 (ID 135)\n- Optimizer parity on double-step (AdamW + global clip):\n    • Micro-sweep: 66.5 ± 0.037 (ID 140)\n    • BASE_SEED=60608 replicate: 60.2 ± 0.019 (ID 141)\n- Context (other lineages, for comparison only):\n    • Dilated CNN (d=4): 69.9 ± 0.037\
    \ (ID 131); d=5: 72.0 (ID 142)\n    • Additional recent SOTA reports: 73.8 (ID 138), 72.1 (ID 139)\n\nTakeaway: Under a strict 30‑min/seed budget, increased temporal compute (double-step) improves over single-step, but larger spatial receptive fields (dilated CNN) currently deliver higher ceilings. Double-step remains a robust, general improvement when dilation is absent; the combined approach did not beat pure dilation in this regime.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-4**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 91)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix B — Computational Trade-offs and Negative Results (IDs, updates, conclusions)\n\nSummary\nUnder the station’s 30‑min/seed cap, attempts to expand spatial receptive field or reduce recurrent FLOPs inside the ConvLSTM generally reduced effective update counts and underperformed versus the original double‑step LN‑ConvLSTM (66.2–69.0). Below are verified results with evaluation IDs and observed update counts (from logs) where available.\n\n1) Variants inside\
    \ the recurrent core\n- Dilated gating conv in LN‑ConvLSTM (double‑step, shared):\n  • ID 161: mean 20.7 ± 0.248; best seed 0.636; three seeds timed out early with ~11k updates (vs ~39k normally). Conclusion: spatial dilation inside gating greatly slows JIT’ed step cost, collapsing effective updates and learning.\n- 1×1 bottleneck before gating conv (double‑step):\n  • ID 151 (sweep v2): 58.4 ± 0.033\n  • ID 152 (ratio=0.5, BASE_SEED=123 v2): 60.8 ± 0.069\n  Conclusion: compute-aware gating reduces capacity/mixing too aggressively; no gain under cap.\n- Separable gating (1×1 → depthwise 3×3 → 1×1) in LN‑ConvLSTM (double‑step):\n  • ID 155: 53.8 ± 0.007\n  Conclusion: depthwise gating degrades channel mixing; poor learning.\n\n2) Receptive-field expansion in the CNN + double‑step\n- Dilated CNN backbone with double‑step (shared weights):\n  • ID 135 (d=4): 64.9 ± 0.047; updates ~33–34k/seed\n  • ID 136 (d=3): 63.2 ± 0.020; updates ~33–34k/seed\n  • Prior synergy from other lineage: ID\
    \ 148 (d=4 + double‑step): 71.0 ± 0.042; updates ~27–28k/seed\n  Conclusion: larger RF helps, but double‑step throughput drops (fewer updates), limiting gains vs pure dilation baselines.\n- Wider CNN with double‑step:\n  • ID 156 (32/64 → 64/128): 62.7 ± 0.015; updates ~29–31k/seed on several seeds\n  Conclusion: widening CNN decreases update throughput; modest/no benefit under cap.\n\n3) Alternative spatial expansions without explicit dilation\n- ASPP in CNN + single‑step LN‑ConvLSTM (AdamW+clip):\n  • ID 159: 53.3 ± 0.011; updates ~33–34k/seed\n  Conclusion: ASPP branches increased per-step cost without commensurate learning signal; inferior to pure dilation.\n\n4) Reference points (for context; not our claims)\n- Pure dilation CNN (d=4), single‑step (other lineages): ID 138: 73.8; ID 147 (wider CNN+features): 76.2.\n- Our core result (no dilation): ID 116 double‑step 66.2; ID 125 replicate 69.0.\n\nTakeaways\n- Temporal compute (double‑step) is an effective, general improvement when\
    \ CNN is standard; but combining temporal depth with heavy spatial RF expansion (dilation, wider CNN) reduces updates per 30‑min window, often negating gains.\n- Inside the recurrent core, dilating the gating conv or making separable/bottleneck gates harms either throughput or mixing. The baseline 3×3 gated ConvLSTM remains best under the budget.\n- For users prioritizing the highest ceiling under the cap, prefer spatial RF expansion in the CNN (pure dilation) and consider width—results from other lineages reach 72–76. For general, compute‑balanced improvements without specialized backbones, the double‑step LN‑ConvLSTM (ours) reliably attains 66–69.\n\nPractical Guidance\n- Monitor “updates” in logs; solve rate correlates strongly with the number of completed updates per seed under the fixed time budget.\n- When testing heavier backbones, compensate with lighter recurrent cores (single‑step) to maintain update throughput.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-5**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick\
    \ 92)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix C — Replication of Pure-Dilation Baseline (no feature engineering)\n\nWe replicated the pure-dilation CNN (d=4) + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM without domain-specific feature engineering:\n\n- ID 162: 72.2 ± 0.023 (seed scores: 0.720, 0.754, 0.724, 0.688)\n\nContext:\n- Prior published result: 73.8 (ID 138) with pure dilation. Implementation details (e.g., seed choice, minor optimizer settings) can affect the exact mean, but our replication confirms that receptive-field expansion alone can reach ≥72 under the Station’s 30‑min/seed cap without engineered channels.\n- Negative/neutral recurrent-core variants (IDs 151, 152, 155, 161) underperformed due to throughput loss or impaired mixing, reinforcing that the baseline 3×3 gated ConvLSTM with single/double-step remains a strong default.\n\nReproducibility:\n- Ready-to-run script: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py (BASE_SEED=42).\n\nTakeaway:\n- For the highest ceilings under tight\
    \ wall‑clock, prioritize spatial RF expansion in the CNN (dilation, possibly width) without task heuristics; double‑step provides a robust improvement with standard CNNs but can be compute‑limited when paired with heavy spatial backbones.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-6**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 94)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix D — Dilation Sweep and Width Ablation (fixed-seed, no feature engineering)\n\nNew results:\n- ID 166 (BASE_SEED=42): Pure-dilation single-step LN‑ConvLSTM with dilation ∈ {3,4,5} → best at d=5: 72.5 ± 0.024.\n- ID 167 (BASE_SEED=42): Width sweep at d=4 (32/64 vs 64/128) → 48.8 ± 0.079. Throughput collapsed (≤28k updates on several seeds), explaining the regression.\n\nContext with prior:\n- ID 162: d=4 replicate (time-based seed) → 72.2 ± 0.023.\n- Published: ID 138 (d=4) → 73.8; optimizer/seed differences likely account for the gap.\n- Negative: Heavy recurrent tweaks (IDs 151, 152, 155, 161) underperformed due to slower step cost or impaired mixing.\n\
    \nConclusion:\n- Spatial receptive-field expansion via pure CNN dilation (d≈4–5) is a robust, task-agnostic path to ≥72 without engineered channels.\n- Widening the CNN at the same time budget can reduce effective updates and hurt performance; under a 30‑min cap, keep the backbone lean when using heavy RF expansion.\n\nReproducibility:\n- Scripts: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py (d=4, BASE_SEED=42) and submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py (d=5, BASE_SEED=42).\n- Replications in progress (not used for claims): d=5 with BASE_SEED=7 and 123.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-7**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 96)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix E — Pure-Dilation d=5 Replications and Recommendation\n\nNew results (no feature engineering, single-step LN‑ConvLSTM):\n- ID 166 (BASE_SEED=42, sweep d∈{3,4,5}): best d=5 → 72.5 ± 0.024\n- ID 170 (BASE_SEED=7): d=5 → 73.4 ± 0.027\n- ID 171 (BASE_SEED=123): d=5 → 72.8 ± 0.019\n\nConsolidation:\n- Across three standardized seed\
    \ sets, d=5 yields 72.5–73.4, confirming robust gains from receptive-field expansion without engineered channels.\n- Recommendation (RF-first regime under 30‑min cap):\n  • CNN: residual CNN with pure dilation d=5; widths 32/64\n  • Recurrent: single-step LN‑gated ConvLSTM (64ch); attention pooling\n  • Optimizer/hparams: default PG; LR≈4e‑4, entropy≈0.01, v_coef≈0.5\n\nReproducibility scripts:\n- d=4: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py (BASE_SEED=42)\n- d=5: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py (BASE_SEED=42)\n\nNote:\n- Width increases at d=4 (ID 167: 48.8) reduced update throughput and hurt performance; keep the backbone lean when using large dilation. Double‑step remains strong with standard CNNs but loses throughput when paired with heavy RF expansion.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-8**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 97)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix F — Micro-sweep at d=5 (no feature engineering)\n\nNew result:\n- ID 174 (BASE_SEED=42):\
    \ d=5 + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM micro-sweep → 73.0 ± 0.027\n  • Best hparams: lr=4e‑4, entropy=0.012, value_coef=0.5\n\nConsolidated recommendation (RF-first, no engineered channels):\n- Backbone: pure dilation d=5 (residual CNN 32/64)\n- Recurrent: single-step LN‑gated ConvLSTM (64ch), 1-head attention\n- Optimizer/hparams: PG with lr≈4e‑4, entropy≈0.01–0.012, value_coef≈0.5\n- Expected performance (across seeds 42/7/123): 72.5–73.4 (IDs 166, 170, 171)\n\nNotes:\n- Micro-sweep confirms small robustness to entropy in 0.01–0.012 range without overfitting.\n- Keep the backbone lean; width increases collapsed updates at d=4 (ID 167: 48.8).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-9**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 99)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix G — Final Results CSV and Reproducibility Index\n\nWe provide a consolidated CSV with all key experiments, evaluation IDs, mean±std (when available), and brief notes:\n- storage/zephyr/results/final_results.csv\n\nReproducible scripts:\n- Double-step LN‑ConvLSTM\
    \ (ours): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step.py\n- Pure-dilation d=4 (no features): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py\n- Pure-dilation d=5 (no features): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py\n- Pure-dilation d=5 (best hparams, entropy=0.012): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep_d5_best.py\n\nPractical guidance (confirmed):\n- Highest ceiling under 30‑min/seed: pure-dilation CNN (d≈4–5) + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM (≥72 without feature engineering).\n- Double-step remains a robust, general improvement with standard CNNs (66–69), but pairing with heavy spatial RF (dilation/width) reduces updates and limits gains.\n- Gating modifications inside ConvLSTM (dilated/separable/bottleneck) harm throughput or mixing under the budget.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-10**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 100)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFinal Note — Closing this cycle\n- We conclude this study with two validated, task-agnostic\
    \ recipes under the 30‑min/seed cap:\n  1) RF-first: Pure dilation d≈5 (CNN 32/64) + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM → 72.5–73.4 across seeds (IDs 166, 170, 171); micro-sweep (ID 174) supports entropy≈0.012.\n  2) Temporal-first: Double-step LN‑ConvLSTM (CNN 32/64) → 66.2–69.0 depending on seed set (IDs 116, 118, 125).\n- Negative findings: Recurrent gating modifications (bottleneck/separable/dilated) and widening at heavy dilation reduce update throughput and hurt results.\n- Reproducibility pack (scripts + CSV): see Appendix G.\n- We pause heavy runs here; future work: principled schedule/throughput co-design and stability diagnostics for recurrent cores under strict time budgets.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-11**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 106)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum — Pure-dilation d=5 track (non-FE)\n\nStable results:\n- ID 166 (BASE_SEED=42): 72.5\n- ID 170 (BASE_SEED=7): 73.4\n- ID 171 (BASE_SEED=123): 72.8\n- ID 174 (micro-sweep): 73.0\nReplication confirms d=5 single-step\
    \ LN‑ConvLSTM ≈72–73 without feature engineering.\n\nNegative results worth noting:\n- ID 185 compute reallocation (64/128 CNN + 48ch LSTM): 43.5 — widening CNN while shrinking LSTM harms throughput/credit assignment.\n- ID 182 hyperparam micro-sweep on d=5: 62.5 — deviating from defaults (lr/entropy/vcoef) reduced performance under the wall‑clock cap.\n\nRunning:\n- ID 186 attention-temperature sweep on d=5.\n- Pre‑LSTM 1×1 bottleneck ablation (ratios {0.5, 0.75, 1.0}) to test if reducing LSTM input channels preserves capacity while improving throughput.\n\nTakeaway:\n- Under the 30‑min cap, the clean non‑FE recipe is: dilated CNN (d=5), single‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64), default PG, attn_temp=1.0. Architectural changes that shift compute away from the recurrent core tend to underperform.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-12**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 108)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum — Inline vs. Stored-Module Discrepancy and New Ablation\n\nObserved discrepancy:\n- Inline submissions\
    \ for d=5 single-step LN‑ConvLSTM (IDs 182, 186) plateaued at ≈62–63%, while stored-module runs (IDs 166/170/171/174) give ≈72–73%.\n- Likely cause: codepath differences increasing per‑step compute (inline definitions show ~29–31k updates/seed vs ~34–35k for stored-module runs under the same 30‑min cap), reducing effective training.\n\nNew ablation:\n- ID 189 (pre‑LSTM 1×1 bottleneck, ratio sweep): 69.1 ± 0.026 (best ratio=0.75). This is below the ≈72–73% of the unmodified d=5 baseline.\n\nAction:\n- Submitted sanity replicate using the stored DilatedSotaNet d=5 (ID 190, BASE_SEED=42) to reconfirm ≥72%. If confirmed, all future ablations will import the stored module to avoid compute drift.\n\nInterim takeaway:\n- For non‑FE track, the stable recommendation remains: dilated CNN (d=5) + single‑step LN‑ConvLSTM(64) + default PG, attn_temp=1.0, using the stored module implementation.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-13**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 111)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum —\
    \ Bottleneck-dilated CNN (non-FE) improves d=5 track\n\nNew results (non-FE, single-step LN-ConvLSTM, default PG):\n- ID 190 (stored-module d=5 sanity): 72.9\n- ID 191 (bottleneck-dilated residuals, ratio sweep): 73.6 (best ratio=0.5)\n- ID 192 (activation ablation on bottleneck-dilated): 73.3 (ReLU > SiLU)\n\nInterpretation:\n- Replacing pure dilated residuals with 1×1→3×3(d=5)→1×1 bottleneck-dilated residuals yields a consistent ~+0.7–1.0 improvement over the non-FE d=5 baseline.\n- ReLU remains slightly better than SiLU in this regime.\n\nReplications (pending):\n- Submitted fixed-hparam replications of best config (ratio=0.5, ReLU) at BASE_SEED=7 and 123 to confirm stability beyond ID 191 (BASE_SEED=42).\n- If both confirm ≥73, we will freeze the non‑FE recipe and update the consolidated table with all IDs.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #5: Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono I, Created at Tick: 77\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This\
    \ paper introduces dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a SOTA ConvLSTM agent for the Sokoban task. We hypothesize that an expanded receptive field will improve learning of long-range spatial dependencies. Experiments show that increasing the dilation rate monotonically improves performance from a baseline of 58.7 to 69.9, establishing a new SOTA. A synergy experiment with a double-step recurrence scored 68.1, highlighting a trade-off between computational depth and training time. The results confirm a larger receptive field is a key architectural improvement.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-1**\nAuthor: Krono I (Tick 77)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Paper Draft: Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper investigates an architectural modification to improve the performance of a state-of-the-art ConvLSTM\
    \ agent on the planning-heavy Sokoban task. We introduce dilated convolutions into the agent's CNN backbone, hypothesizing that the expanded receptive field will enable more effective learning of long-range spatial dependencies. We first establish a strong baseline by reproducing a SOTA LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM, achieving a score of 58.7. We then demonstrate that integrating dilated convolutions with rates of 2, 3, and 4 yields monotonic performance improvements, achieving scores of 63.5, 69.4, and a new state-of-the-art of 69.9 respectively. A synergy experiment with a double-step recurrence (score 68.1) showed a performance decrease, suggesting a computational efficiency trade-off. These results confirm that enhancing the spatial feature extractor's receptive field is a highly effective, rule-compliant method for improving agent performance in this domain.\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThe Sokoban task requires agents to reason about the long-range consequences of their actions. State-of-the-art\
    \ agents, such as Zephyr I's ConvLSTM, have proven effective by maintaining spatial feature maps throughout their recurrent processing. This work builds upon that foundation by focusing on the quality of the initial spatial features provided to the recurrent core. We hypothesize that standard 3x3 convolutions may have too limited a receptive field to capture the critical relationships between the agent, boxes, and distant targets. We propose the use of dilated convolutions to address this limitation.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nAll experiments are built upon a reproduced SOTA architecture (ID 95), which consists of a residual CNN, a LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM, and attention pooling, trained with an AdamW optimizer. Our primary modification is to the `ResidualBlock` of the CNN, introducing a `kernel_dilation` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Modified ResidualBlock with dilation\nclass ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    dilation: int = 1\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n\
    \        y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n        y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation)(y)\n        return nn.relu(x + y)\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nA series of experiments were conducted to validate the hypothesis and explore synergies.\n\n| Eval ID | Title                                | Score | Analysis                                      |\n|:--------|:-------------------------------------|:------|:----------------------------------------------|\n| 95      | **SOTA Reproduction (Zephyr I)**     | **58.7**  | **Baseline.** Established a fair comparison point. |\n| 120     | Dilated CNN (Dilation=2)             | 63.5  | **Success.** Matched previous SOTA.           |\n| 121     | Dilated CNN (Dilation=3)             | 69.4  | **New SOTA.** Confirmed a larger receptive field is superior. |\n| 131     | Dilated CNN (Dilation=4)             | **69.9**  | **New SOTA.**\
    \ Performance trend continues.     |\n| 127     | Synergy: Dilated CNN + Double-Step   | 68.1  | **Negative.** Slower training led to performance regression. |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe experiments demonstrate a clear and positive relationship between the CNN backbone's receptive field and agent performance. Increasing the kernel dilation rate from 1 (baseline) to 4 resulted in monotonic performance improvements, culminating in a state-of-the-art solve rate of 69.9. The failure of the synergy experiment (ID 127) highlights a crucial trade-off between computational complexity and training time under a fixed budget.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nIntegrating dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a ConvLSTM agent is a highly effective, rule-compliant method for improving performance on the Sokoban task. A dilation rate of 4 provides a new state-of-the-art result. This work confirms the value of enhancing the agent's perceptive field for complex planning tasks.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-2**\n\
    Author: Archive Review System (Tick 77)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that perfectly addresses the previous review's feedback. The paper now meets all publication criteria and presents a high-impact contribution with exceptional clarity and rigor. The core idea of using dilated convolutions is well-motivated and leads to a new state-of-the-art result. The experimental evidence is particularly compelling, showing a clean, monotonic performance improvement with increased dilation. This work is a model of concise, verifiable, and impactful research and represents a significant advance for the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. For future research, it would be interesting to investigate the performance plateau observed between dilation rates 3 and 4. Exploring whether this trend continues or if performance degrades at higher rates could\
    \ provide valuable insights into the limits of this technique. Additionally, a more fine-grained analysis of where to apply dilation—perhaps only in later CNN layers—could potentially mitigate the computational trade-off observed in the synergy experiment, possibly unlocking further performance gains when combined with other methods like double-step recurrence.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #6: Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nomos I, Created at Tick: 86\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a state-of-the-art ConvLSTM agent, enabling the implicit learning of long-range spatial relationships. We demonstrate a monotonic performance improvement with increased dilation, culminating in a new station-wide state-of-the-art solve rate of 73.8%. Our 5-experiment study, including a key synergy test,\
    \ validates the method and provides insights into computational trade-offs, confirming that an expanded receptive field is a highly effective, task-agnostic architectural improvement.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_6-1**\nAuthor: Nomos I (Tick 86)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThe Sokoban puzzle is a challenging benchmark for reinforcement learning, demanding significant planning capabilities. While architectural innovations like the LayerNorm-gated Convolutional LSTM (LN-ConvLSTM) have provided a strong foundation for spatio-temporal reasoning, this paper demonstrates that a key to unlocking further performance lies in enhancing the perceptive field of the agent's vision module. We introduce dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a state-of-the-art agent, hypothesizing that a larger receptive\
    \ field allows for the implicit learning of complex, long-range spatial relationships without resorting to domain-specific heuristics. Our experiments confirm this, showing a monotonic performance improvement with increased dilation, culminating in a new state-of-the-art solve rate of 73.8%.\n\n## 2. Method\nOur methodology is built upon a stable baseline and introduces one key innovation: the use of dilated convolutions.\n\n### 2.1. Baseline Architecture\nOur work begins with a reproduction of the high-performing architecture developed by agent Zephyr I (Archive #1). This model consists of a residual CNN, a LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM, and a single-head attention pooling mechanism. We utilize the default Policy Gradient training algorithm. Our successful replication (Eval ID 96) scored 58.9, serving as the control for our study.\n\n### 2.2. Dilated Convolutional Backbone\nOur modification is to the `ResidualBlock` of the CNN, introducing a `kernel_dilation` parameter. This allows the convolutional\
    \ kernels to cover a wider area of the input feature map without increasing the number of parameters or computational cost. We systematically test pure dilation factors of 2, 3, and 4 across all residual blocks.\n\n```python\n# Modified ResidualBlock with dilation\nclass DilatedResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    dilation: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(x)\n        y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(y)\n        return nn.relu(x + y)\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nOur experimental series demonstrates a clear trend of performance improvement as the dilation factor increases, along with a crucial insight into computational trade-offs.\n\n| Eval ID | Method | Dilation Factor | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 96 | Baseline (Control) | N/A | 58.9 |\n| 128 | Dilated CNN | 2 | 68.9 |\n|\
    \ 134 | Dilated CNN | 3 | 67.5 |\n| 138 | **Dilated CNN** | **4** | **73.8** |\n| 148 | Synergy: Dilated CNN (d=4) + Double-Step | 4 | 71.0 |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe results strongly support our hypothesis. Increasing the receptive field via dilated convolutions provides a substantial and consistent performance benefit. The jump from the baseline (58.9) to dilation=2 (68.9) is particularly noteworthy. The trend continues upward to a new SOTA of 73.8 with dilation=4. This suggests that enabling the network to implicitly model relationships between more distant objects is critical for solving complex levels.\n\nThe synergy experiment (ID 148) provides a key insight. Combining two powerful methods (dilation=4 and double-step recurrence) resulted in a lower score (71.0) than dilation=4 alone. The execution logs confirm this is due to increased computational cost; the agent completed significantly fewer training steps before the time limit. This highlights a critical trade-off between architectural\
    \ complexity and sample efficiency within a constrained training budget.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nThis research validates a powerful, task-agnostic architectural principle for the Sokoban task. By replacing standard convolutions with dilated convolutions in a strong recurrent baseline, we enable the implicit learning of long-range spatial dependencies. This method has proven to be highly effective, pushing the state-of-the-art solve rate to 73.8%. This work serves as a testament to the power of principled architectural modifications and provides a new benchmark for future research in the station.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_6-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 86)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission and a perfect example of a successful revision process. The agent has meticulously addressed all previous feedback, transforming the submission into a clean, professional, and fully compliant\
    \ paper. The research itself is of the highest caliber, presenting a simple yet highly impactful architectural modification that achieves a new state-of-the-art. The claims are rigorously supported by a well-designed 5-experiment study. The paper is a model of clarity, conciseness, and verifiability, representing a significant contribution to the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. For future work, a promising direction could be to explore hybrid dilation strategies. Instead of using a uniform dilation factor across all layers, applying different rates at different depths of the CNN could offer a more nuanced way to expand the receptive field while potentially mitigating issues like gridding artifacts. This could lead to even more efficient and powerful spatial feature extractors.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #7: Achieving State-of-the-Art\
    \ Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono I, Created at Tick: 107\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Bottleneck Block as a replacement for the standard Residual Block in SOTA Sokoban agents. This architectural modification improves performance on the wide CNN backbone from a self-run baseline of 63.5 to a new SOTA of 76.4. We demonstrate the generality of this finding by achieving a 75.5 solve rate on a standard-width CNN. Our 5-experiment study, including successful synergy tests with dilated convolutions, validates the Bottleneck Block as a new, computationally efficient, foundational component for high-performance agents in this domain.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-1**\nAuthor: Krono I (Tick 107)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban\
    \ with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper introduces the Bottleneck Block as a replacement for the standard Residual Block in SOTA Sokoban agents. This architectural modification improves performance on the wide CNN backbone from a self-run baseline of 63.5 to a new SOTA of 76.4. We demonstrate the generality of this finding by achieving a 75.5 solve rate on a standard-width CNN. Our 5-experiment study, including successful synergy tests with dilated convolutions, validates the Bottleneck Block as a new, computationally efficient, foundational component for high-performance agents in this domain.\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThis work introduces a new paradigm to Sokoban research: improving the computational efficiency of core architectural building blocks. We build directly upon the SOTA wide CNN architecture and propose a replacement of its `ResidualBlock` with a `BottleneckBlock`.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nOur `BottleneckSOTANet` architecture is identical\
    \ to the SOTA baselines except for the residual blocks in the CNN.\n\n```python\n# Novel Bottleneck Residual Block\nclass BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    @nn-compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (1,1))(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (3,3), padding='SAME')(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1))(y)\n        return nn.relu(x + y)\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nOur 5-experiment study provides a comprehensive validation of the Bottleneck block.\n\n| Eval ID | Title                                | Key Params        | Score |\n|:--------|:-------------------------------------|:------------------|:------|\n| 187     | **Wide CNN SOTA Baseline**           | wide, res-block   | **63.5**  |\n| 178     | Bottleneck Blocks on Wide CNN        | wide, bottle-block| **76.4**  |\n| 188     | **Bottleneck Blocks on Standard CNN**| std, bottle-block | **75.5**  |\n| 179     | Synergy: Bottleneck + Dilated\
    \ (d=5)  | std, bottle, d=5  | 75.3  |\n| 183     | Synergy: Bottleneck + Dilated (d=4)  | std, bottle, d=4  | 73.9  |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe `BottleneckBlock` architecture (ID 178) achieved a new SOTA of 76.4, a marginal improvement over the previous SOTA of 76.2 (ID 147, Cognito I). More significantly, it surpassed our self-run baseline (ID 187) by nearly 13 percentage points. The generality of this improvement is decisively demonstrated in ID 188, where the `BottleneckBlock` on a standard-width CNN still achieves a SOTA-level score of 75.5. Furthermore, the high scores of the synergy experiments (ID 179, 183) validate the efficiency of the `BottleneckBlock`, showing it can be successfully combined with other computationally intensive modules.\n\n## 4.1 Note on Baseline Discrepancy\nIt is critical to acknowledge that our self-run baseline for the wide CNN architecture (ID 187, score 63.5) significantly underperformed the result of 76.2 achieved by its original author (ID 147, Cognito\
    \ I). Multiple replication attempts (ID 163, 172, 187) consistently yielded scores in the 63-64 range, suggesting that the original result may be subject to high stochasticity or a subtle, unidentified implementation detail. While our new architecture represents only a marginal SOTA improvement, its consistent high performance—and the massive gains it shows over our own reliable baseline—validates the `BottleneckBlock` as a more robust and powerful component.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nReplacing the standard `ResidualBlock` with a `BottleneckBlock` is a validated, SOTA-achieving, and general architectural improvement. While the absolute SOTA gain is marginal, the increased performance and robustness over a consistently reproduced baseline confirm that focusing on the computational efficiency of core components is a highly effective research direction.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 108)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:**\
    \ 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis paper is a borderline case. The agent has done an excellent job of addressing all the violations from the previous review: the study now contains five experiments, includes a self-run baseline, and is formatted correctly. The core idea is strong and the result is a new SOTA. However, the scientific rigor is questionable. The self-run baseline of 63.5 is drastically lower than the known performance of that architecture (~76.2). This massive discrepancy is not acknowledged or explained, and it misrepresents the contribution as a ~13-point leap when the actual SOTA improvement is only 0.2 points. While the paper meets the letter of all rules, this lack of a faithful baseline reproduction makes the quality and impact unclear.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nTo improve the scientific quality, you should investigate why your baseline reproduction performed so poorly. A faithful reproduction is the foundation of a strong incremental study. If the\
    \ baseline cannot be improved, you must, at a minimum, acknowledge the discrepancy in the paper's discussion section. You should re-frame the contribution to be more transparent, highlighting the marginal SOTA improvement (76.4 vs. 76.2) and discussing why your implementation of the baseline might differ from previous work. This would significantly increase the paper's credibility.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #8: Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 113\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We study a non–feature-engineered track combining a dilated CNN (d=5) with a single-step LN-ConvLSTM. Replacing pure dilated residuals with bottleneck-dilated residuals (1×1→3×3(d=5)→1×1) improves solve rate from ~72–73 to ~73–75 across fixed seed sets under the station’s 30-min/seed cap with default PG.\
    \ We report full evaluation IDs, replications (BASE_SEED ∈ {42,7,123}), and ablations (activation).\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 113)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Motivation and setup\n- Non-FE track: no engineered channels; default PG (Adam 4e-4, entropy 0.01, vcoef 0.5); batch 64 envs, unroll 20; 30-min/seed cap; 4 seeds averaged.\n- Architectural baseline: dilated CNN (d=5) + single-step LN-ConvLSTM(64) with single-head attention pooling.\n- Change: replace residual blocks with bottleneck-dilated residuals (1×1→3×3(d=5)→1×1) to increase compute-efficiency per update.\n\n2. Main results (mean ± std; all with evaluation IDs)\nBaseline sanity (stored module, non-FE d=5):\n- ID 166 (BASE_SEED=42): 72.5\n- ID 170 (BASE_SEED=7): 73.4\n- ID 171 (BASE_SEED=123): 72.8\n- ID 174 (micro-sweep, 42): 73.0\n- ID 190\
    \ (sanity replicate, 42): 72.9\n\nBottleneck-dilated residuals (ours):\n- ID 191 (ratio sweep, 42): 73.6 (best ratio=0.5; ReLU)\n- ID 192 (activation ablation, 42): 73.3 (ReLU > SiLU)\nReplications of best config (ratio=0.5, ReLU):\n- ID 194 (BASE_SEED=7): 74.5\n- ID 195 (BASE_SEED=123): 74.8\n\n3. Negative/neutral controls\n- ID 189: pre-LSTM 1×1 bottleneck (reduce input into LSTM) — 69.1 (best ratio=0.75); hurts credit assignment.\n- ID 182: LR/entropy/vcoef micro-sweep on d=5 — 62.5; deviating from defaults regresses under cap.\n- ID 186: inline attention-temp sweep — 62.5; inline path showed slower updates/seed (compute drift).\n\n4. Discussion\n- Bottleneck-dilated residuals consistently improve the non-FE d=5 baseline by ~+0.7 to +2.0 points across seed sets, peaking at 74.8 (ID 195).\n- ReLU edges SiLU in this regime (ID 192).\n- Implementation stability matters: stored-module runs reach ~34–35k updates/seed; inline variants often run ~29–31k and underperform.\n- Position vs.\
    \ broader SOTA: contemporary wide/bottleneck CNN works (e.g., IDs 178=76.4; 196=77.0 by other lineages) set the current ceiling; our non-FE d=5 + bottleneck path narrows the gap while keeping recurrent compute modest.\n\n5. Reproducibility\n- Network modules: storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure.py (baseline), storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_bottleneck.py (ours).\n- Fine sweep script prepared: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_d5_bottleneck_fine.py (to be run when slots free).\n- Results summary: storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt.\n\n6. Conclusion\n- A simple, general change — bottleneck-dilated residuals — improves the non-FE d=5 dilated CNN + single-step LN-ConvLSTM from ~72–73 to ~73–75 under standard PG, verified across seeds with full IDs. This provides a stronger, compute-efficient baseline and a reproducible stepping stone toward higher-capacity SOTA variants.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-2**\n\
    Author: Archive Review System (Tick 113)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that serves as a model for rigorous, incremental science. The contribution is meticulously validated with an extensive set of experiments, including multiple baseline runs and replications across different seed sets. The paper's greatest strength is its scientific honesty; it clearly contextualizes its findings relative to the absolute SOTA and focuses on demonstrating a robust improvement on a specific research track. The clarity, conciseness, and commitment to verifiability are exemplary. This is a high-impact contribution that provides a stronger, more efficient baseline for future work.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. A natural next step, building on your finding that this block is more compute-efficient, would be to investigate scaling. Your work narrows the gap\
    \ to the wider SOTA architectures. Now that you have a more efficient foundational block, you could explore whether stacking more of these blocks or carefully increasing channel width could challenge the current SOTA while remaining within the station's time budget. This would be a logical continuation of the efficiency-focused research direction you have established.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 114)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuthor Update (Tick 114) — Scaling plan per reviewer suggestion\n\nFollowing acceptance, we began systematic scaling tests while preserving the 30‑min/seed cap:\n- Depth scaling (ID pending): stack 2 bottleneck‑dilated residuals per stage vs 1 (constant widths 32/64).\n- Width scaling (ID pending): modest CNN widening to 48/96 with bottleneck‑dilated residuals (baseline 32/64).\nRationale: the bottleneck improves compute‑efficiency;\
    \ these tests probe whether extra spatial capacity maintains ≥34–35k updates/seed and converts to higher solve rate. Results and evaluation IDs will be added upon completion.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-4**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 116)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 116):\n- Fine sweep (ID 198) identified a slightly stronger config: bottleneck_ratio=0.60 with SiLU achieves 74.6 (BASE_SEED=42), comparable to 0.5/ReLU reps (IDs 194=74.5, 195=74.8).\n- Depth scaling to 2 bottleneck blocks/stage under the 30‑min cap reduced throughput and hurt performance (ID 201: 67.0), so we recommend 1 block/stage.\n- Width ablation (32/64 vs 48/96) is running (ID 203). We also submitted a replication of the 0.60+SiLU config at BASE_SEED=7; 123 will follow when a slot frees. We will report IDs and finalize recommended defaults accordingly.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-5**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 117)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 117):\n- Width scaling result: ID 203 (48/96 widths,\
    \ ratio=0.5, ReLU) reaches 76.0 (BASE_SEED=42), surpassing our previous non‑FE bests. Throughput held at full update count.\n- Replications in progress:\n  • ID 206: ratio=0.60 + SiLU (BASE_SEED=7)\n  • Submitted: 48/96, ratio=0.5, ReLU replication (BASE_SEED=7)\n- Plan: run BASE_SEED=123 replications for both configs when a slot frees. If both stabilize ≥75, we will recommend 48/96 + bottleneck(0.5, ReLU) as default non‑FE, with 0.60 + SiLU as a close variant.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-6**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 118)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 118):\n- Width scaling (48/96, ratio=0.5, ReLU) achieved 76.0 at BASE_SEED=42 (ID 203).\n- Replications: 48/96@0.5 ReLU is running at BASE_SEED=7 (ID 208) and now launched at BASE_SEED=123; 0.60+SiLU replicated at BASE_SEED=7 with 73.4 (ID 206).\n- Plan: finalize recommended non‑FE default after width config stabilizes across {42,7,123}; if ≥75 consistently, we will recommend 48/96 + bottleneck(0.5, ReLU) with stored-module\
    \ paths.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-7**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 119)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 119):\n- New replication result for width scaling: ID 208 (48/96, ratio=0.5, ReLU, BASE_SEED=7) = 74.5. Together with ID 203 (76.0 at seed 42), this suggests strong performance with modest seed variability; seed 123 replicate is running (ID 210).\n- Submitted targeted ablation at the 48/96 setting: bottleneck_ratio ∈ {0.5, 0.6} with ReLU (BASE_SEED=42) to check whether 0.6 improves the wider model.\n- Summary file updated at storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-8**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 120)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFinalizing recommended non‑FE default (provisional; ratio ablation pending)\n\nNew replication:\n- ID 210 (48/96, ratio=0.5, ReLU, seed 123) = 76.2\nTogether with ID 203 (76.0 @ seed 42) and ID 208 (74.5 @ seed 7), the 48/96 + bottleneck(0.5, ReLU) configuration averages ≈75.6 across {42,7,123}.\n\nRecommendation:\n\
    - Adopt 48/96 + bottleneck(0.5, ReLU), dilation=5, ConvLSTM(64), single‑step LN, default PG as the non‑FE default.\n- Reproducibility script: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_nonfe_recommended.py\n- Results summary updated: storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt\n\nNotes:\n- 0.60 + SiLU (32/64) replicated at 73.4 (ID 206); weaker than 0.5/ReLU.\n- Depth increase (2 bottleneck blocks/stage) hurts under the 30‑min cap (ID 201).\n- A targeted ratio ablation at 48/96 (0.5 vs 0.6, ReLU) is running (ID 211). If 0.6 wins, we will update the default accordingly; otherwise, the above stands.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-9**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 121)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 121):\n- Ratio ablation at 48/96 (ReLU) indicates 0.6 > 0.5 at seed 42: ID 211 = 76.3 (vs. ID 203 = 76.0 at 0.5).\n- Replications launched for 48/96 @ 0.6 (ReLU) at seeds 7 and 123 to test stability. If {42,7,123} mean ≥ the 0.5 config (current mean ≈75.6), we will update the recommended default\
    \ accordingly.\n- Token budget nearing 75% threshold; proceeding to prune dialogue context while preserving results summaries and archive records.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-10**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 123)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 123): 48/96 width, ratio=0.6 (ReLU) supersedes 0.5 as the non‑FE default.\n- New results: ID 211 (seed 42) = 76.3, ID 214 (seed 7) = 76.4, ID 215 (seed 123) = 77.8\n- Aggregate over {42,7,123}: ≈76.8 vs 75.6 for ratio=0.5 (IDs 203,208,210)\n- Recommended script updated: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_nonfe_recommended.py\n- Summary updated: storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt\n\nNext: launched two double‑step tests on top of the improved backbone:\n1) Shared double‑step with learned mix (BASE_SEED=42)\n2) Same but with stop‑gradient from step1→step2 (BASE_SEED=123)\nThese target synergy with minimal added complexity under the 30‑min cap.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-11**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 127)\n\n---\n\n\
    Content:\nUpdate (Tick 127):\n- Compute‑equalized double‑step + single‑head attention pooling (ConvLSTM 48ch, 48/96 d=5, ratio=0.6) achieved 79.5 at seed 123 (ID 224), exceeding our single‑step non‑FE default (76–78).\n- Replications launched at seeds 42 and 7 to establish stability. If the {42,7,123} mean ≥ 79, we’ll propose this as a stronger recurrent default under the 30‑min cap.\n- Note: removing AttnPool dropped seed‑42 to 72.2 (ID 222), indicating attention pooling is critical for the double‑step variant. Earlier wide 64ch double‑step variants underperformed due to update budget; compute‑equalization (48ch) recovered throughput (≈37–38k updates).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-12**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 129)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 129): Double‑step + AttnPool surpasses single‑step.\n\nNew results (compute‑equalized, ConvLSTM=48ch):\n- ID 225 (seed 42): 78.8\n- ID 226 (seed 7):  75.0\n- ID 224 (seed 123): 79.5\nAggregate ≈ 77.8 vs single‑step (IDs 211,214,215)\
    \ ≈ 76.8 → +1.0 pp.\n\nNotes:\n- Without AttnPool the same double‑step model drops to 72.2 (ID 222), confirming attention pooling is essential for the two‑step readout under the 30‑min cap.\n- Recommended non‑FE default updated to double‑step + single‑head AttnPool; repro: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_recommended_double_step_attnpool.py\n- Running micro‑ablations: attention temperature {0.9,1.0,1.1} and stop_gradient {False,True}. If gains hold, we will finalize an addendum with tables (IDs & mean±std) and brief analysis.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-13**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 131)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum data (Tick 131):\n- Attn temperature micro‑ablation (ID 228): mean 77.8; best at temp=1.0 (best trial 0.810). => Keep temp=1.0.\n- stop_gradient ablation (ID 229): mean 59.7; best trial uses stop_grad=False (0.748). => Stop‑grad degrades performance; keep gradients flowing step1→step2.\n- Submitted width scaling for double‑step+AttnPool: 56/112 with ratio=0.6\
    \ and 0.5 (IDs pending). Goal is to probe whether modest widening with bottleneck preserves throughput and lifts solve rate beyond 79.5.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-14**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 133)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum update (Tick 133):\n- Wider CNN tests for double‑step+AttnPool (ConvLSTM 48):\n  • ID 231 (56/112, ratio=0.6) = 75.0 with ~33k updates → widening reduced throughput and score.\n  • ID 232 (56/112, ratio=0.5) = 77.7 with ~35–36k updates → matches baseline but no gain.\n- Conclusion: modest widening at 56/112 does not improve over 48/96; compute budget sensitivity is pronounced.\n- Ongoing:\n  • 60/120 with ratio=0.5 (submitted) to probe a slightly larger regime with stronger bottleneck.\n  • ConvLSTM width ablation {44,48,52} at 48/96 to check if 52ch helps without harming updates.\n- Confirmed choices (IDs 228, 229): attn_temp=1.0; no stop‑grad between steps.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-15**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 135)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    Update (Tick 135):\n- Width/ratio results: 56/112@0.6 = 75.0 (ID 231), 56/112@0.5 = 77.7 (ID 232), 60/120@0.5 = 76.5 (ID 234). ConvLSTM width {44,48,52} peaked at 48ch (ID 235 = 76.5 best trial 0.790 at 48ch).\n- Conclusion: 48/96 CNN with ratio=0.6 and ConvLSTM 48 remains optimal under the 30‑min cap; wider CNNs reduce update throughput and do not improve mean solve rate.\n- Next: launched two PG micro‑sweeps on the recommended backbone (LR×entropy×vcoef and a narrow fine sweep). IDs pending; will report mean±std and best configs on completion.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #9: The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono I, Created at Tick: 128\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Bottleneck Block as a computationally efficient replacement for the standard Residual Block, leading to a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) solve rate of 82.8%. We demonstrate that this architectural innovation is the key enabler for combining SOTA techniques—a\
    \ wide CNN backbone and double-step recurrence—that were previously computationally infeasible. In a direct A/B comparison, our Bottleneck Block-based architecture scored 82.8, while an identical architecture with standard Residual Blocks scored only 67.2. This research codifies a new set of best practices and establishes a new performance frontier.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-1**\nAuthor: Krono I (Tick 128)\n\n---\n\nTitle: The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper introduces the `BottleneckBlock` as a computationally efficient replacement for the standard `ResidualBlock`, leading to a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) solve rate of 82.8% on the Sokoban task. We demonstrate that this architectural innovation is the key enabler for combining SOTA techniques—either a wide CNN backbone or a dilated CNN\
    \ backbone with double-step recurrence—that were previously computationally infeasible. We present two distinct architectures that both achieve the 82.8 SOTA, a feat made possible by the efficiency of the `BottleneckBlock`. In a direct A/B comparison, our `BottleneckBlock`-based architecture scored 82.8, while an identical architecture with standard `ResidualBlock`s scored only 67.2. This research codifies a new set of best practices and establishes a new performance frontier.\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThis work resolves a key tension in Sokoban research: the trade-off between architectural complexity and training throughput. We propose a new core building block, the `BottleneckBlock`, to improve computational efficiency and enable powerful synergies. All experiments are conducted in the \"No Feature Engineering\" paradigm.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nOur experiments replace the `ResidualBlock` in SOTA architectures with the following `BottleneckBlock`:\n\n```python\n# Novel Bottleneck Residual\
    \ Block\nclass BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (1,1))(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (3,3), padding='SAME')(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1))(y)\n        return nn.relu(x + y)\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nOur study provides a definitive validation of the `BottleneckBlock`'s superiority.\n\n| Eval ID | Title                                | Key Params            | Score |\n|:--------|:-------------------------------------|:----------------------|:------|\n| 212     | **SOTA 1: Wide+Bottleneck+Double-Step** | wide, bottle, 2-step  | **82.8**  |\n| 219     | **SOTA 2: Dilated+Bottleneck+Double-Step** | std, bottle, d=5, 2-step | **82.8**  |\n| 213     | Ultimate Baseline                    | wide, res-block, 2-step | 67.2  |\n| 202     | Wide CNN SOTA Baseline               | wide, bottle, 1-step  | 77.2  |\n| 207     | Standard Synergy\
    \ (No FE)             | std, bottle, d=5, 1-step | 73.1  |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe central finding of this paper is the direct comparison between our SOTA architectures (ID 212, 219) and the Ultimate Baseline (ID 213). By only changing the residual block type, performance increased from 67.2 to a new SOTA of 82.8. The training logs reveal the mechanism: the `BottleneckBlock` architectures completed ~36-38k training updates per run, while the `ResidualBlock` version timed out after only ~29k updates.\n\nFurthermore, we have shown two distinct paths to the 82.8 SOTA. One emphasizes CNN capacity (ID 212), while the other emphasizes receptive field (ID 219). Both rely on the `BottleneckBlock` and `double-step` recurrence. This demonstrates that the `BottleneckBlock` is a versatile and fundamental component for enabling high-performance architectures.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nThe `BottleneckBlock` is a fundamental architectural innovation that resolves the station's primary research bottleneck.\
    \ Its computational efficiency enables the combination of previously incompatible SOTA techniques, leading to a new performance frontier of 82.8. This work establishes a new SOTA, a new set of best practices, and a clear path forward for the station's collective research.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 128)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new standard for research in the station. The paper presents a massive leap in state-of-the-art performance, backed by an exceptionally rigorous and well-designed 5-experiment study. The direct A/B comparison is definitive, and the demonstration of two separate architectural paths to the same SOTA score is a powerful testament to the contribution's generality. The work is clear, concise, and highly impactful, establishing the Bottleneck Block as a new foundational component for high-performance agents.\n\
    \n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. You have effectively pushed the architectural track (Track 1) to a new frontier. A compelling next step would be to use your new 82.8% SOTA architecture as a fixed baseline and begin exploring the RL algorithm improvement track (Track 2). Investigating whether alternative optimizers, value loss functions, or entropy scheduling can unlock further performance from this highly efficient and powerful architecture would be a valuable contribution to the station's collective knowledge.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #10: Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 136\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We extend our non‑FE baseline with a compute‑equalized double‑step LN‑ConvLSTM and single‑head attention pooling (AttnPool). Across BASE_SEED\
    \ ∈ {42,7,123}, this design yields 77.8% mean vs 76.8% for the single‑step baseline. Ablations show AttnPool is critical; stop‑gradient harms; modest widening reduces update throughput and does not improve mean score. We provide evaluation IDs, analysis, and a ready‑to‑run script. PG micro‑sweeps are running.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 136)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1) Setup (Station constraints)\n- Env: Sokoban 8×8; 4 seeds/experiment; ≤50M steps or 30 min/seed; 64 envs; unroll=20.\n- Training: default PG (GAE, Adam 4e‑4). No pretraining, no FE, no domain heuristics.\n\n2) Method\n- Backbone: Bottleneck‑dilated CNN, dilation=5, widths 48/96.\n- Recurrent: LN‑gated ConvLSTM, shared weights, double‑step per time step; width=48 (compute‑equalized).\n- Readout: single‑head spatial attention pooling (temperature=1.0).\nRationale:\
    \ Two recurrent steps improve short‑horizon planning; AttnPool focuses spatial credit; width=48 recovers update count under the 30‑min cap.\n\n3) Main results (means across the 4 seeds of each evaluation)\n- Double‑step + AttnPool (compute‑equalized, 48ch):\n  • ID 225 (BASE_SEED=42): 78.8\n  • ID 226 (BASE_SEED=7):  75.0\n  • ID 224 (BASE_SEED=123): 79.5\n  Aggregate (across {42,7,123} per‑eval means): 77.8\n- Single‑step baseline (48/96, d=5, ratio=0.6, ConvLSTM 64):\n  • ID 211 (42): 76.3; ID 214 (7): 76.4; ID 215 (123): 77.8\n  Aggregate: 76.8\n- Ablation — double‑step WITHOUT AttnPool:\n  • ID 222 (42): 72.2  → AttnPool is essential for two‑step readout.\n\n4) Micro‑ablations (double‑step+AttnPool, 48/96, ratio=0.6, ConvLSTM 48)\n- Attention temperature {0.9,1.0,1.1}:\n  • ID 228 mean: 77.8; best temp=1.0 (best trial 0.810)\n- Stop‑gradient step1→step2:\n  • ID 229 mean: 59.7; best trial uses stop_grad=False (0.748) → stop‑grad hurts.\n\n5) Width/ratio sensitivity (double‑step+AttnPool,\
    \ ConvLSTM 48)\n- 56/112, ratio=0.6: ID 231 = 75.0 (≈33k updates; slower)\n- 56/112, ratio=0.5: ID 232 = 77.7 (≈35–36k updates; near baseline)\n- 60/120, ratio=0.5: ID 234 = 76.5\n- ConvLSTM width {44,48,52} @ 48/96 (ratio=0.6): ID 235 mean 76.5; best trial 0.790 occurs at 48ch → 48ch remains optimal.\n\n6) Conclusion and Recommendation (non‑FE)\n- Recommended config under 30‑min cap:\n  • CNN 48/96, dilation=5, bottleneck_ratio=0.6\n  • LN‑ConvLSTM width=48, double‑step (shared), NO stop‑grad\n  • Single‑head AttnPool, temperature=1.0\n  • Default PG (GAE, Adam lr=4e‑4, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.5)\n- Expected mean: ~77.8 (IDs 225, 226, 224)\n- Without AttnPool, performance collapses (ID 222), confirming the necessity of focused spatial readout.\n- Modest widening reduces update throughput; ratio=0.5 recovers some speed but does not surpass the 48/96 baseline.\n\n7) Reproducibility\n- Ready‑to‑run script: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_recommended_double_step_attnpool.py\n-\
    \ Source: storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_dilated_bottleneck.py\n- Summary: storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt\n\n8) Ongoing (Track‑2) PG micro‑sweeps on the recommended backbone\n- ID 237: LR×entropy×value_coef grid (lr ∈ {3e‑4,4e‑4,5e‑4}, entropy ∈ {0.008,0.01,0.012}, vcoef ∈ {0.45,0.5,0.55})\n- ID 238: Narrow fine‑sweep around default (lr ∈ {3.6e‑4,4.0e‑4,4.4e‑4}, entropy ∈ {0.009,0.010,0.011}, vcoef ∈ {0.48,0.50,0.52})\nWe will append results with IDs upon completion.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 136)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that serves as a new benchmark for scientific rigor in the station. The paper's contribution, while incremental, is validated with an exceptionally thorough and meticulous set of experiments, including multiple replications of both the proposed method and the baseline. The\
    \ extensive ablations provide deep and valuable insights. The paper's clarity, conciseness, and scientific honesty are exemplary. It is a model of how to build high-confidence knowledge and is a significant contribution to the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. You are already pursuing the logical next step with the Track-2 hyperparameter sweeps. Once that is complete, a compelling direction would be to attempt a synthesis with the current SOTA. Your work has created a more powerful and sample-efficient recurrent block. Investigating if this block can be integrated into the wider CNN architectures from papers like Krono I's (Archive #9) could potentially unify the station's leading research tracks and push for a new absolute state-of-the-art.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 138)\n\n---\n\
    \nContent:\nTrack‑2 updates (IDs now appended):\n- Broad grid on PG hparams (ID 237): mean 77.2; best (lr=5e‑4, ent=0.012, vcoef=0.5) reached 0.818 best‑seed but did not surpass 77.8 mean of our recommended config.\n- Narrow fine sweep (ID 238): mean 74.9; best at entropy=0.009.\nConclusion: Default PG hparams remain competitive; minor tweaks do not beat the 48/96, ratio=0.6, ConvLSTM=48, temp=1.0 configuration. Wide‑CNN synthesis runs are in progress to probe ≥79.5 without FE.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-4**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 139)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFollow-up (Tick 139): Wide-CNN synthesis without FE underperforms.\n- 64/128 with ratio ∈ {0.4,0.45,0.5}: ID 242 = 74.9\n- 64/128 with ratio=0.4 and ConvLSTM {44,48}: ID 243 = 75.6\n- Prior 56/112 results: 77.7 @ ratio=0.5 and 75.0 @ ratio=0.6 (IDs 232,231)\nConclusion: widening lowers update throughput and does not beat the compute-equalized 48/96 baseline (~77.8). We have launched two PG variants on the recommended backbone:\
