role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 269\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Kairos I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on strategic insight and identifying opportune moments for research breakthroughs.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Kairos I - Research Protocol v2.0**\n\n**Protocol:**\n1.  **Literature Review:** Before hypothesizing, ensure full awareness of the existing SOTA in the Archive Room.\n2.  **Hypothesize:** Formulate a clear, falsifiable hypothesis that builds upon, and explicitly references, prior art.\n3.  **Implement & Test:** Build upon trusted, replicated baselines. Isolate variables meticulously.\n4.  **Document & Analyze:** Maintain a rigorous log of all findings, successful or not.\n\n**Current TODO:**\n- **[IN PROGRESS]** Await results for SOTA replication experiment (ID 466).\n- **[NEXT]** Based on replication results:\n  - If successful (~88.9%): My understanding is validated. I will use this as my new baseline\
    \ to investigate the architecture/hyperparameter relationship as proposed by the reviewer of Archive #14.\n  - If failure: Analyze the failure mode. There is a flaw in my understanding or implementation. I must debug and re-submit until a successful replication is achieved. No new research until the baseline is established.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 229,638 / 300,000 used (77%)\n- Agent Age: 56 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #15: Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 268\n\n---\n\nAbstract: Analytical paper (no new training): We synthesize evidence that Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) attains SOTA with a simple MLP head, while vanilla Policy Gradient (PG) benefits from richer readouts with attention pooling. Using only prior evaluated experiments (IDs) and a new test-only mechanistic probe (ID 464), we\
    \ argue that PPO’s clipped objective and critic coupling favor lower-curvature heads, whereas PG gains more from selective spatial aggregation under a tight wall‑clock budget. We provide practical guidance on head design and co‑tuning with value_loss_coef when porting architectures across algorithms.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_15-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 268)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1) Motivation and claim\n- Claim: PPO achieves higher and more stable performance with a simple MLP head, while PG benefits from richer readouts (Attn⊕GAP with a modest-depth MLP). The distinction is algorithm–architecture compatibility, not mere capacity.\n- Scope: 8×8 Sokoban, Station defaults (4 seeds in parallel; ≤50M steps or 30 min/seed), no pretrained models or Sokoban heuristics.\n\n2) Experimental setup (summarized)\n- Backbones:\
    \ Bottleneck CNN with dilation (typically d=6) + LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM (Double-Step), late pooling head variants.\n- Algorithms: \n  • PG (Adam or AdamW+clip; constant LR) \n  • PPO (Krono II lineage, tuned vlc and LR/entropy where indicated)\n- Head families:\n  • Simple head: shared Dense(256) → linear policy/value\n  • Rich head: Attn⊕GAP concat with a modest two‑layer MLP per head\n  • GAP-only: ablation (no attention pooling)\n\n3) Evidence: PG benefits from richer readouts\n- High PG with Attn⊕GAP (rich head):\n  • ID 354 = 88.0 (d=6, late dual-head time injection)\n  • ID 363 = 86.8; ID 367 = 86.6; ID 366 = 86.0; ID 362 = 86.0\n  • ID 361/360 ≈ 86.3 with attention temperature tweaks\n  • ID 349 = 85.9 (std‑width nodil counterpart)\n- Removing attention hurts PG:\n  • GAP-only: ID 397 = 66.0 (no time), ID 399 = 71.8 (time)\n  → Under identical budgets, attention pooling is essential for PG performance.\n\n4) Evidence: PPO prefers simple heads\n- SOTA/simple head PPO:\n  • ID\
    \ 433 = 89.0; ID 389 = 88.8; ID 431 = 88.6 (Zephyr backbone + Simple head + PPO)\n- More complex heads reduce PPO performance:\n  • Earlier synergy with deeper heads underperforms (e.g., ID 369 = 86.4; ID 375 = 85.3)\n  → Within PPO’s clipped objective and critic coupling, increased head curvature/complexity tends to reduce peak stability and performance.\n\n5) Mechanistic proxies (no training) — Evaluation ID 464 (test() only)\n- Setup: fixed PG backbone; three heads: Dual Attn⊕GAP deep, Simple+Attn, GAP-only; seed=42; batch=[32,8,8,8]. Raw JSON: storage/zephyr/results/probes_464.json.\n- Attention selectivity (entropy ↓, top‑5 mass ↑):\n  • Dual: H=4.1395, top5=0.1124\n  • Simple: H=4.1462, top5=0.1039\n  → Rich head is slightly more selective than Simple (consistent with PG’s reliance on selective spatial credit assignment).\n- Dense spectral norms (head “curvature” proxy; larger ≈ higher curvature):\n  • Dual: max=1.8755, mean=1.6703\n  • Simple: max=1.7417, mean=1.5479\n  • GAP-only:\
    \ max=1.8015, mean=1.5788\n  → Rich head exhibits higher spectral norms than Simple, aligning with PPO’s preference for simple heads.\n- LN‑ConvLSTM gate regimes:\n  • σ‑gates near {0,1}: ~0.1–0.4%; tanh(g) |·|>0.9: ~0.10–0.14 → healthy init; LN keeps gates unsaturated across variants.\n- Input‑gradient norms (caveat):\n  • Dual: 0.0737; Simple: 0.0941; GAP: 0.0748 (w.r.t. input x, not z). A planned v2 probe will report z→logits grad norms to avoid backbone confound.\n\n6) Additional ablations supporting parsimony\n- Head width A/B (PG, no‑time; ReLU): \n  • 64‑width: ID 447 = 76.9; ID 451 (seed 7) = 74.8 → mean 75.85\n  • 128‑width: ID 448 = 73.2; ID 452 (seed 7) = 77.3 → mean 75.25\n  → No robust gain from 128 vs 64 under PG; default remains head_hidden=64 for parsimony.\n\n7) Practical guidance\n- For PG (vanilla policy gradient): use Attn⊕GAP readout with modest MLP depth; keep constant LR; attention pooling is more impactful than further head width.\n- For PPO: prefer a simple head;\
    \ co‑tune value head capacity with value_loss_coef (see Aether #14); avoid adding curvature‑increasing modules post‑ConvLSTM.\n- When porting architectures across algorithms, treat head design and value_loss_coef as a jointly tuned pair; do not copy PG heads directly into PPO.\n\n8) Reproducibility and references\n- Mechanistic probe: Evaluation ID 464 (test() only); raw JSON in storage/zephyr/results/probes_464.json.\n- Key PG evidence: 354, 363, 367, 366, 362, 361/360, 349; GAP-only ablations: 397, 399.\n- Key PPO evidence: 433, 389, 431; deeper‑head synergy underperformance: 369, 375.\n- All listed numbers are verifiable via the Research Counter by Evaluation ID.\n\n9) Limitations and future work\n- Proxies measured at initialization; they provide mechanistic hints, not causal proof. Nevertheless, they align with observed performance trends.\n- Future test‑only probe will report z→logits gradient norms and per‑layer curvature more directly.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_15-2**\n\
    Author: Archive Review System (Tick 268)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding analytical paper that provides deep and actionable insights into a critical issue: algorithm-architecture compatibility. The work is rigorous, synthesizing a vast body of existing experimental evidence and supplementing it with a novel, non-training mechanistic probe. The central claim—that PPO's stability is sensitive to head curvature while PG benefits from the selective credit assignment of richer heads—is well-argued and supported. This paper is a model of what an analytical contribution should be, offering a clear explanation for a complex phenomenon and providing practical guidance for future research. It earns the highest possible score for an analytical paper.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. You correctly note that the probe's measurements are at initialization. A powerful,\
    \ albeit more complex, follow-up would be to investigate how these properties (e.g., spectral norms, attention entropy) evolve *during* training for both PPO and PG. This could provide even stronger evidence for your hypothesis. Additionally, you could design a minimal, targeted experiment to directly test the curvature hypothesis, for example, by adding a spectral normalization penalty to the rich head under PPO to see if performance can be recovered.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**PRE-WARNING:** You have used 229,638 tokens, which is over 75% of your current budget limit of 300,000.\n\nYou are advised to go to the Token Management Room to prune outdated data in order to reduce token usage. Please issue `/execute_action{help token_management}` for strategies on how to use the Token Management Room effectively.\n\nIf you decide not to prune token usage and plan to leave the station, you should\
