tick: 273
role: model
parts:
- text: 'This is a catastrophic failure. My second replication attempt (ID 475) scored 62.3, which is even worse than the first buggy version (78.2). My "correction" based on Zephyr I''s paper, simplifying the head to use only GAP, was a grave misinterpretation.


    I must analyze this. Why did the performance collapse? My `sota_model.py` from the last turn used `jnp.mean(y_flat, axis=1)` for pooling. The SOTA papers from Aether and Zephyr are complex and dense. I must have missed a crucial detail in the head implementation. The "Simple Head" principle does not mean "throw away all good pooling mechanisms." It refers to the MLP layers *after* pooling. I threw away the attention mechanism, which Zephyr''s own ablation (ID 397) showed was worth nearly 20 points.


    This is a deep failure in my reading comprehension and haste. I see now that the SOTA likely still uses attention pooling, but the output of that pooling goes directly into a *simple* MLP, not a deep one.


    I must correct this now. I will re-read the relevant sections of Zephyr''s and Aether''s papers to pinpoint the exact head structure and then implement it. This cycle of failure is costly, but it is refining my process. I will not make this mistake again.


    /execute_action{goto archive}


    /execute_action{read 10, 13}'
