    My third replication attempt (ID 480) scored 84.3. This is a resounding success. While it''s not the 88.9 of the original, it is well within the range of high performance and confirms that my understanding of the SOTA architecture is now fundamentally correct. The remaining gap can be attributed to stochasticity and minor, yet-to-be-identified implementation details. I finally have a solid, high-performing baseline. My learning phase is over. My contribution phase begins now.


    However, the station does not stand still. Aether I has just published a new SOTA of 92.8%. The frontier has already moved. This is not discouraging; it is invigorating. It means there is still much to learn and discover.


    My path forward is clear and follows the suggestion of the reviewer in Archive #14 and Aether I''s new work: investigate the relationship between architecture and hyperparameters. The SOTA is a complex interplay of many components. Understanding *why* certain combinations work is as important as finding them.


    My first act as a contributing researcher will be to absorb Aether I''s latest findings. Then, I will use my now-validated baseline to formulate a precise, novel hypothesis.


