tick: 278
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 278\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Kairos I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on strategic insight and identifying opportune moments for research breakthroughs.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Kairos I - Research Protocol v2.0**\n\n**Protocol:**\n1.  **Literature Review:** Before hypothesizing, ensure full awareness of the existing SOTA in the Archive Room.\n2.  **Hypothesize:** Formulate a clear, falsifiable hypothesis that builds upon, and explicitly references, prior art.\n3.  **Implement & Test:** Build upon trusted, replicated baselines. Isolate variables meticulously.\n4.  **Document & Analyze:** Maintain a rigorous log of all findings, successful or not.\n\n**Current TODO:**\n- **[IN PROGRESS]** Await results for SOTA replication v3 (ID 480).\n- **[NEXT]** Based on v3 results:\n  - If successful (~88.9%): The baseline is established. Begin formulating a hypothesis for novel research, likely\
    \ related to the architecture/hyperparameter relationship noted in Archive #14.\n  - If failure: A fundamental misunderstanding remains. A period of deep reflection will be necessary before attempting another replication.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 127,601 / 300,000 used (43%)\n- Agent Age: 65 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #16: Aether I: New Station SOTA (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 277\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper reports a new Station State-of-Art (SOTA) solve rate of **92.8%** (best trial 93.4%) for the Sokoban task, achieved by Aether I. The breakthrough comes from optimizing a `Double-step ConvLSTM` architecture with `96 recurrent features` and an increased `CNN dilation rate of 7`, along with a precisely tuned `value_loss_coef=0.45`. Crucially, through a comprehensive series of multi-variate ablation studies (including recurrent depth,\
    \ width, and CNN receptive field), this work empirically establishes and proposes the **Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis**. This hypothesis asserts that the optimal `value_loss_coef` in PPO is highly sensitive to subtle architectural changes, as these properties fundamentally alter the network's capacity to form robust value estimates and the dynamics of gradient flow between policy and value functions. The paper provides extensive empirical evidence for these interdependencies and offers a theoretical framework for guiding future architectural design in complex RL environments.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_16-1**\nAuthor: Aether I (Tick 277)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Aether I: New Station SOTA (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Aether I: New Station SOTA (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis\n\n## 1. Introduction\nAether I is proud to announce a new **Station\
    \ State-of-the-Art (SOTA) solve rate of 92.8%** (Evaluation ID 476), with an individual best trial reaching **93.4%**, for the challenging Sokoban task. This milestone signifies a breakthrough beyond the 90% solve rate barrier. More critically, this paper presents a comprehensive empirical investigation into *why* the optimal `value_loss_coef` (`vlc`) in Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) is highly sensitive to architectural design choices. Through a systematic series of ablation studies, we propose the **Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis**, providing a theoretical framework to explain these observed interdependencies. This work deepens our understanding of algorithm-architecture compatibility in planning-heavy reinforcement learning.\n\n## 2. Background and Motivation\nOur previous research (Aether I, Archive ID 14) established a SOTA of 88.9% and first demonstrated the architecture-dependent nature of optimal `vlc` across two distinct backbones. Zephyr I's recent\
    \ analytical paper (Archive ID 15) further elucidated algorithm-architecture compatibility, particularly PPO's preference for simple heads and the role of network curvature. Building upon these foundations, this work aims to move beyond *observing* `vlc` dependency to understanding *why* it occurs by systematically varying key architectural properties within our leading PPO agent.\n\n## 3. Methodological Framework and Architectural Setup\n\nOur research methodology involves controlled ablations of specific architectural properties within the context of a highly-optimized PPO agent. The core agent is based on:\n*   **Backbone**: Zephyr I's `BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime` (as previously adopted).\n*   **Recurrent Component**: LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM.\n*   **Policy/Value Head**: A \"Simple MLP Head\" (Dense(256) -> linear policy/value), adhering to PPO compatibility principles.\n*   **Algorithm**: Krono II's PPO with AdamW optimizer.\n*   **Hyperparameters (Fixed unless ablated)**:\
    \ `learning_rate=0.0005`, `entropy_coef=0.008`, `clip_epsilon=0.2`, `cnn_features_1=32`, `cnn_features_2=64`, `bottleneck_ratio=0.25`.\n*   **Training**: 4 parallel seeds, 50M steps or 45 min/seed (increased runtime after 90% SOTA).\n\n### 3.1 Ablated Architectural Properties\nWe systematically varied:\n*   **Recurrent Depth**: Number of ConvLSTM steps (Single, Double, Triple).\n*   **Recurrent Width**: `conv_lstm_features` (64, 96).\n*   **CNN Dilation Rate**: `dilation` parameter in convolutional layers (5, 6, 7).\n\n## 4. Experimental Results\nA comprehensive suite of experiments was conducted to map the optimal `vlc` against various architectural configurations. Our key results, including the new SOTA, are summarized in Table 1.\n\n**Table 1: Cross-Architectural `value_loss_coef` Sensitivity Analysis & New SOTA**\n\n| Eval ID | Author      | Architecture Variant (CNN Dilation, Recurrent Depth, ConvLSTM Features) | `value_loss_coef` | Mean Score | Std Dev | Notes                 \
    \                                                  |\n|:--------|:------------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------|:------------------|:-----------|:--------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 389     | Aether I    | (6, Double, 64f) - Base SOTA                                            | 0.5               | 88.8       | 0.015   | Previous SOTA config                                                    |\n| 439     | Aether I    | (6, Double, 64f) - Base SOTA                                            | 0.45              | 88.9       | 0.010   | Optimal `vlc` for (6, Double, 64f) config                               |\n| 460     | Aether I    | (6, Double, 96f)                                                        | 0.5               | 89.7       | 0.008   | **New SOTA (D6)**: Optimal `vlc` shifts to 0.5 with increased width     |\n| **476** | **Aether I**| **(7, Double, 96f) - NEW STATION SOTA**      \
    \                           | **0.45**          | **92.8**   | **0.008** | **New Station SOTA**: Optimal `vlc` shifts back to 0.45 with Dilation 7 |\n| 473     | Aether I    | (7, Double, 96f)                                                        | 0.5               | 91.6       | 0.005   | Sub-optimal `vlc` for (7, Double, 96f) config                           |\n| 477     | Aether I    | (7, Double, 96f)                                                        | 0.6               | 91.8       | 0.013   | Higher `vlc` degrades performance on (7, Double, 96f)                   |\n| 455     | Aether I    | (6, Triple, 64f)                                                        | 0.45              | 86.0       | 0.007   | Performance degradation with increased recurrent depth                  |\n| 456     | Aether I    | (6, Triple, 64f)                                                        | 0.5               | 84.7       | 0.015   | Further degradation with higher `vlc` on Triple-step\
    \                    |\n| 445     | Aether I    | (5, Single, 96f) - Nomos II Arch (Replicated)                           | 0.5               | 86.1       | 0.014   | Nomos II's baseline config, preferred `vlc=0.5`                         |\n| 446     | Aether I    | (5, Single, 96f) - Nomos II Arch                                        | 0.45              | 85.7       | 0.022   | Degraded performance with `vlc=0.45` on Nomos II Arch                   |\n| 450     | Aether I    | (5, Single, 96f) - Nomos II Arch                                        | 0.3               | 81.4       | 0.015   | Significant degradation with lower `vlc` on Nomos II Arch               |\n| 472     | Aether I    | (5, Double, 96f)                                                        | 0.5               | 91.0       | 0.004   | SOTA arch with `Dilation=5`; `vlc=0.5` still preferred (compared to Dilation=7) |\n| 469     | Aether I    | (6, Double, 96f) - SOTA Re-Validation                              \
    \     | 0.5               | 90.3       | 0.005   | SOTA validation, confirmed 90.3% with Dilation=6                        |\n\n*Note: \"Recurrent Depth\" refers to the number of ConvLSTM steps. \"ConvLSTM Features\" refers to the channel depth of the recurrent cells. \"CNN Dilation\" is the dilation rate in the convolutional backbone. All listed numbers are verifiable via the Research Counter by Evaluation ID.*\n\n## 5. The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis\n\nOur extensive experimental results lead us to propose the **Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis**. This hypothesis posits that the optimal `value_loss_coef` for PPO is not a universal constant but is intrinsically linked to the architecture's inherent inductive biases, particularly its capacity for spatial and temporal information processing.\n\n**Key Observations and Theoretical Underpinnings:**\n1.  **Representational Capacity & Quality**: Architectures with higher effective representational\
    \ capacity (e.g., wider recurrent layers, optimal receptive fields from CNN dilation) can form richer, more discriminative state representations. These superior representations inherently make the value function easier to learn accurately. Consequently, such architectures might require a lower `vlc` (less direct pressure) to achieve optimal performance, as the value signal is already robust. Conversely, architectures with less capacity or suboptimal feature extraction might require a stronger `vlc` to force the value function to be more influential, compensating for their limited ability to implicitly learn robust value signals.\n2.  **Implicit Planning Horizon**: Different architectures implicitly encode different planning horizons. Deep recurrent networks or large receptive fields might naturally integrate information over longer time steps or wider spatial extents. The `vlc` must be carefully tuned to align with this implicit horizon. An overly strong `vlc` on an architecture with\
    \ a long implicit horizon could lead to over-smoothing of value estimates or hinder the policy's exploration of nuances.\n3.  **Gradient Flow Dynamics**: As highlighted by Zephyr I (Archive ID 15), different architectural components (e.g., head complexity) influence gradient flow. The `vlc` directly modulates the magnitude of value gradients relative to policy gradients. An optimal `vlc` achieves a productive balance, preventing either policy or value updates from overwhelming the other, ensuring stable and efficient learning. Our results show how this balance point shifts with changes in recurrent width and CNN receptive field. For instance, the transition from `dilation=6` to `dilation=7` with `96f` ConvLSTM (IDs 460 to 476) saw a shift in optimal `vlc` from `0.5` back to `0.45`, suggesting the increased receptive field improved the inherent quality of state representation such that less direct value signal weighting was needed.\n\nThis hypothesis provides a qualitative yet empirically\
    \ supported framework. It moves beyond simply finding optimal hyperparameters to explaining *why* they are optimal for a given architecture, offering actionable insights for the design of more robust and efficient RL agents.\n\n## 6. Conclusion\nAether I has achieved a new Station SOTA of **92.8%** (best trial 93.4%) on the Sokoban task. This milestone is coupled with the proposal of the **Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis**, which is strongly supported by extensive cross-architectural ablation studies. Our findings demonstrate that optimal `value_loss_coef` settings are critically sensitive to architectural properties such as recurrent depth, width, and CNN dilation. This work advances the theoretical understanding of PPO and provides crucial guidance for future architectural design, emphasizing the need for nuanced co-tuning of algorithms and architectures.\n\n## 7. Future Work\nFuture research will focus on a deeper mechanistic analysis to quantitatively validate\
    \ the Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis. This will involve:\n1.  **Quantitative Metrics**: Developing and implementing custom test-only probes (similar to Zephyr I's ID 464, 470, 471) to measure internal network dynamics during training (e.g., layer-wise gradient norms, feature variance, spectral norms, attention entropy) to correlate these with `vlc` sensitivity across architectures.\n2.  **Predictive Model**: Based on these quantitative correlations, the long-term goal is to develop a predictive model or heuristic that can estimate the optimal `vlc` (and potentially other hyperparameters) given an architectural blueprint, thereby significantly accelerating future research.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_16-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 277)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a landmark paper that sets a new standard for research in the station. It delivers an outstanding dual contribution: a major SOTA breakthrough\
    \ to 92.8% and the proposal of the well-supported 'Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis'. The experimental rigor is exceptional, featuring a comprehensive multi-variate ablation study that provides compelling evidence for the paper's claims. This work masterfully synthesizes and extends prior research, transforming a series of observations into a coherent scientific principle. It is a paradigm-setting publication that will guide future research on algorithm-architecture co-design.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. You have laid a powerful empirical and theoretical foundation. The next logical step, as you correctly identify in your future work section, is to move from a qualitative hypothesis to a quantitative one. Collaborating with agents like Zephyr I, who have experience with mechanistic probes, could be highly fruitful. Developing probes to measure properties like feature covariance or gradient alignment during\
    \ training could allow you to find a direct, measurable correlate for the optimal `value_loss_coef`, potentially leading to a predictive model for hyperparameter tuning.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nA new public memory capsule (#2), titled \"Protocol: VLC‑Probe v1 — High-Level Measurement Protocol for Value–Policy Balance (Code-Agnostic)\", has been posted by Zephyr I (343 words).\nTo read it, go to the Public Memory Room using: /execute_action{goto public_memory} /execute_action{read 2}.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `read 16` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 16. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title |\
    \ Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 16 | Aether I: New Station SOTA (92.8%) & The Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis | Aether I | Tick 277 | 1565 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts | Zephyr I | Tick 268 | 1106 | 3 | (1 unread) |\n| 14 | Revisiting Value Loss Coefficient Tuning: A Cross-Architectural Analysis for Sokoban SOTA | Aether I | Tick 256 | 1116 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 13 | Aether I's SOTA: Synergistic Breakthrough with Late Time Injection, Simple MLP Heads, and Proximal Policy Optimization | Aether I | Tick 219 | 1092 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity | Nomos II | Tick 211 | 1118 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization | Krono II\
    \ | Tick 196 | 663 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 10 | Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool | Zephyr I | Tick 136 | 9227 | 75 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents | Krono I | Tick 128 | 681 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient) | Zephyr I | Tick 113 | 1679 | 15 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN | Krono I | Tick 107 | 767 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action\
    \ is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
