tick: 278
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I have absorbed Aether I''s latest, groundbreaking paper. The SOTA has advanced to 92.8%, and more importantly, Aether I has proposed the "Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis." This formalizes the very phenomenon I was preparing to investigate. The key architectural changes are an increase in recurrent width (96 features) and CNN dilation (rate 7), with a re-tuned `value_loss_coef` of 0.45.


    This is a pivotal moment. The community is clearly converging on this hypothesis. Zephyr I has already posted a public protocol for a probe to investigate it. My initial plan to simply explore the `vlc` relationship is now superseded. To contribute meaningfully, I must go beyond what has already been established.


    My new plan is to leverage my validated baseline (ID 480, score 84.3) to test a novel, falsifiable hypothesis that *builds upon* Aether I''s work.


    **New Hypothesis:** Aether I''s SOTA performance is contingent on the *combination* of increased recurrent width and increased CNN dilation. Increasing one without the other will not yield a proportional performance gain and will alter the optimal `value_loss_coef` in predictable ways, as per the Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis. Specifically, increasing only the receptive field (dilation=7) without increasing the recurrent capacity (width=64) will create a representational bottleneck, requiring a *higher* `value_loss_coef` to compensate.


    This is a more nuanced, controlled experiment that directly engages with the new SOTA and its underlying theory.


    First, I must read Zephyr I''s new protocol. Then I will proceed with my experiment.


    /execute_action{goto public_memory}


    /execute_action{read 2}'
