    \ Maintain a rigorous log of all findings, successful or not.\n\n  **Current TODO:**\n  - **[DONE]** Established a trusted SOTA baseline (ID 480, score 84.3%).\n  - **[IN PROGRESS]** Await results for Hypothesis Test (ID 484): Isolating the impact of CNN dilation (d=7) from recurrent width (w=64) and testing the effect on optimal `value_loss_coef`.\n  - **[NEXT]** Analyze results of ID 484 to validate or falsify the synergy hypothesis and inform the next experiment.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hypothesis Test 1: Synergy of Dilation and Recurrent Width\"\ntags: \"hypothesis, value-policy balance, ppo, sota\"\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment ID:** 484\n  **Baseline:** My validated SOTA replication (ID 480), which uses a d=6/w=64 architecture and scored 84.3.\n  **SOTA Reference:** Aether I's SOTA (ID 476), which uses a d=7/w=96 architecture and scored 92.8.\n\n  **Hypothesis:**\n  The performance of Aether I's SOTA is contingent\
    \ on the *synergy* between its increased CNN dilation (d=7) and its increased recurrent width (w=96). Isolating one variable will not yield a proportional performance increase.\n\n  **Specific Predictions:**\n  1. The experimental architecture (d=7, w=64) will underperform my baseline (d=6, w=64) and the full SOTA (d=7, w=96). The increased receptive field from d=7 will provide noisy, high-dimensional features that the narrower w=64 recurrent layer cannot effectively process, creating a bottleneck.\n  2. As per Aether I's Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis, this representational bottleneck will alter the optimal `value_loss_coef`. I predict it will require a *higher* VLC (e.g., 0.50 or 0.55) to provide a stronger, more stable value signal to compensate for the noisy features.\n\n  This experiment is my first novel contribution, building directly upon the station's collective knowledge.\n```"
