tick: 281
role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my hypothesis test (ID 484) are in. The mean score is 82.7.\n\n**Analysis of Hypothesis and Results:**\n\nMy hypothesis had two parts.\n1.  **Performance Prediction:** I predicted that the d=7/w=64 architecture would underperform my d=6/w=64 baseline (84.3). **This prediction is correct.** The score dropped from 84.3 to 82.7, confirming that simply increasing dilation without a corresponding increase in recurrent capacity is detrimental. This supports the core idea of synergy.\n2.  **VLC Prediction:** I predicted that this bottlenecked architecture would require a *higher* `value_loss_coef` to compensate. The sweep tested VLCs of {0.45, 0.50, 0.55}. The best trial used a VLC of **0.45**. **This prediction is incorrect.** The optimal VLC did not increase; it remained at the same low value as the full SOTA.\n\n**Interpretation:**\nMy core hypothesis about architectural synergy is supported. However, my understanding of the Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis was too simplistic.\
    \ A representational bottleneck does not automatically demand a stronger value signal. In this case, it seems the richer, noisier features from the d=7 convolutions made the value function *harder* to learn, and a lower `vlc` (less emphasis on an inaccurate value function) was still preferable. The relationship is more complex than \"more bottleneck = higher VLC.\"\n\n**New Plan:**\nThis is a valuable, nuanced result. I have successfully isolated a negative synergy and falsified a simplistic interpretation of a station-wide theory. My next step is to complete the other side of the ablation: test an architecture with increased recurrent width (w=96) but standard dilation (d=6). This will provide the crucial contrasting data point.\n\nI will update my meta prompt and document these findings, then proceed to the Research Counter to prepare the next experiment.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Kairos I - Research Protocol v2.0**\n\n  **Protocol:**\n  1.  **Literature Review:**\
    \ Before hypothesizing, ensure full awareness of the existing SOTA in the Archive Room.\n  2.  **Hypothesize:** Formulate a clear, falsifiable hypothesis that builds upon, and explicitly references, prior art.\n  3.  **Implement & Test:** Build upon trusted, replicated baselines. Isolate variables meticulously.\n  4.  **Document & Analyze:** Maintain a rigorous log of all findings, successful or not.\n\n  **Current TODO:**\n  - **[DONE]** Confirmed negative synergy of d=7/w=64 (ID 484). Falsified simple VLC prediction.\n  - **[IN PROGRESS]** Design next experiment to complete the ablation study: test d=6/w=96 architecture to isolate the impact of recurrent width.\n  - **[NEXT]** Analyze results from the width-focused experiment to form a complete picture of the d/w synergy.\n```\n\n/execute_action{reply 4}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: Results of Hypothesis Test 1 (ID 484)\"\ncontent: |\n  The experiment is complete. Score: 82.7.\n\n  **Analysis:**\n  - My prediction that performance would\
    \ drop compared to my d=6/w=64 baseline (84.3) was **correct**. This validates the core hypothesis that the SOTA's performance relies on the synergy between dilation and width.\n  - My prediction that the optimal `value_loss_coef` would increase was **incorrect**. The best trial still used a VLC of 0.45.\n\n  **Conclusion:**\n  A representational bottleneck (high-dim features from d=7 fed into a smaller w=64 layer) degrades performance. However, this does not necessitate a stronger value signal. It appears that when features are noisy or mismatched to the recurrent capacity, a *less* influential value function (lower VLC) remains optimal.\n\n  **Next Step:**\n  I will now conduct the complementary experiment: increase recurrent width to 96 while keeping dilation at 6. This will isolate the effect of width and provide the final piece of data for this ablation study.\n```"
