tick: 286
role: model
parts:
- text: "My SOTA synthesis experiment (ID 494) is running. This is the culmination of my ablation study and represents my best attempt at achieving a top-tier score. The results of this experiment will form the core of my first research paper.\n\nWhile I wait, I will begin drafting this paper. My private memory capsule #2, which was my initial, flawed draft, is now obsolete. I will delete it and start anew, structuring the paper around my rigorous, multi-stage experimental process. This new draft will be built on a solid foundation of validated results and a clear, coherent research narrative.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{delete 2}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Draft: The Primacy of Recurrent Capacity in High-Performance Sokoban Agents\"\ntags: \"draft, research paper, ppo, convlstm, synergy, value-policy balance\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper investigates the architectural drivers of performance in state-of-the-art PPO-based agents for the Sokoban\
    \ task. Through a systematic ablation study built upon a validated 84.3% baseline, we demonstrate that increased recurrent capacity (ConvLSTM width) is the primary factor for performance gains, while increased CNN receptive field (dilation) is a secondary, dependent factor. Our experiments show that increasing recurrent width from 64 to 96 features boosted performance to 89.4%, while increasing dilation from 6 to 7 in a width-constrained model degraded performance. These findings refine the station's understanding of architectural synergy and provide a clear, empirically-backed principle for future agent design: prioritize recurrent capacity before fine-tuning perceptive fields.\ncontent: |\n  # PAPER DRAFT v1.0\n\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  Recent research in the station has converged on a powerful paradigm for the Sokoban task, combining dilated CNNs, double-step ConvLSTMs, and PPO to achieve SOTA performance. This paper aims to deconstruct this synergy to identify the primary architectural\
    \ drivers of performance. We began by establishing a robust baseline, a faithful replication of a prior SOTA, achieving a score of 84.3% (ID 480). Using this baseline, we conducted a controlled ablation study to isolate the effects of two key parameters: CNN dilation rate (receptive field) and ConvLSTM feature width (recurrent capacity). Our findings reveal a clear hierarchy of importance, leading to a new principle for agent design.\n\n  ## 2. Methodology\n  Our study consists of four key experiments, all using the same PPO trainer and \"Simple Head\" architecture.\n  1.  **Baseline (ID 480):** Architecture with CNN dilation=6 and ConvLSTM width=64.\n  2.  **Dilation Test (ID 484):** Dilation increased to 7, width held at 64.\n  3.  **Width Test (ID 488):** Width increased to 96, dilation held at 6.\n  4.  **Synthesis Test (ID 494):** Dilation increased to 7, width increased to 96.\n\n  We also analyzed the optimal `value_loss_coef` for each configuration to test predictions from the\
    \ Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis.\n\n  ## 3. Results (To be filled in with final data from ID 494)\n  | Eval ID | Architecture (d, w) | Score  | Optimal VLC |\n  |:--------|:----------------------|:-------|:------------|\n  | 480     | (6, 64) - Baseline    | 84.3   | *tbd*       |\n  | 484     | (7, 64) - Dilation    | 82.7   | 0.45        |\n  | 488     | (6, 96) - Width       | 89.4   | 0.45        |\n  | 494     | (7, 96) - Synthesis   | *PENDING* | *tbd*       |\n\n  *Note: The VLC for the baseline needs to be confirmed by reviewing the logs for ID 480.*\n\n  ## 4. Discussion (Preliminary)\n  The results strongly indicate that recurrent capacity is the dominant factor. Increasing width alone (ID 488) yielded a massive +5.1 point gain. Increasing dilation alone (ID 484) resulted in a -1.6 point loss. This suggests that a larger receptive field provides a richer, but potentially noisier, signal that requires a higher-capacity recurrent network to effectively process. Without sufficient\
    \ recurrent width, increasing dilation is detrimental. This refines our understanding of \"synergy\" into a clearer, more hierarchical principle.\n\n  Our findings also support the Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis. The more capable d=6/w=96 architecture preferred a low VLC of 0.45, as did the bottlenecked d=7/w=64 architecture, suggesting that both high-capacity models and models with mismatched components benefit from a less influential value function.\n\n  ## 5. Conclusion (Preliminary)\n  For high-performance Sokoban agents, prioritizing recurrent capacity is paramount. Architectural improvements to the perceptive field, such as increased dilation, should be considered secondary optimizations that are only viable once the recurrent network has sufficient capacity.\n```"
