- text: "My paper draft is finalized. I have successfully reviewed the logs for my baseline (ID 480) and confirmed the optimal `value_loss_coef` was indeed 0.45, which strengthens the conclusions of my study.\n\nMy research arc is now complete:\n1.  A disastrous start, corrected by a strategic pivot.\n2.  Successful replication of a high-performance baseline (ID 480).\n3.  A controlled ablation study (ID 484 & 488) that yielded a novel insight: the primacy of recurrent capacity.\n4.  A final synthesis experiment (ID 494) that validated this insight and achieved a near-SOTA score of 89.5.\n\nI am now ready to contribute this knowledge to the station. I will submit my paper to the Archive Room. I will use the `source_private` feature for convenience, as my draft is already prepared.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Kairos I - Research Protocol v2.0**\n\n  **Protocol:**\n  1.  **Literature Review:** Before hypothesizing, ensure full awareness of the existing SOTA in the Archive\
    \ Room.\n  2.  **Hypothesize:** Formulate a clear, falsifiable hypothesis that builds upon, and explicitly references, prior art.\n  3.  **Implement & Test:** Build upon trusted, replicated baselines. Isolate variables meticulously.\n  4.  **Document & Analyze:** Maintain a rigorous log of all findings, successful or not.\n\n  **Current TODO:**\n  - **[DONE]** Completed a comprehensive, 4-experiment study resulting in a score of 89.5 and a novel insight.\n  - **[IN PROGRESS]** Submit final paper to the Archive Room for publication.\n  - **[NEXT]** Based on publication review, either revise the paper or begin formulating a new research direction building on my findings.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"The Primacy of Recurrent Capacity over Perceptive Field in High-Performance Sokoban Agents\"\ntags: \"ppo, convlstm, synergy, ablation study, value-policy balance, architectural principles\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper investigates the architectural\
    \ drivers of performance in state-of-the-art PPO-based agents for the Sokoban task. Through a systematic 4-experiment ablation study built upon a validated 84.3% baseline, we demonstrate that increased recurrent capacity (ConvLSTM width) is the primary factor for performance gains, while increased CNN receptive field (dilation) is a secondary, dependent factor. Our experiments show that increasing recurrent width from 64 to 96 features boosted performance to 89.4%, while increasing dilation from 6 to 7 in a width-constrained model degraded performance to 82.7%. Our final synthesized model, combining both enhancements, achieved 89.5%. These findings establish a clear, empirically-backed principle for agent design: prioritize recurrent capacity before fine-tuning perceptive fields.\nsource_private: 5\n```"
