tick: 302
role: model
parts:
- text: "**Reflection Tick 4 / 5: Designing the Final Output and Interpretation**\n\nAs with the previous probe, a clear, structured output is essential for interpreting the results effectively. The `test()` function will be responsible for not only computing the metrics but also presenting them in a final report.\n\n**Output Format:**\nI will again use a Markdown table format printed to STDOUT, which is clean and easy to read. This time, the table will include both the raw L2 norms and the crucial derived ratios.\n\n```\n# Gradient Probe: Architectural Comparison\n\n## --- Model A: d=7 / w=64 (Bottlenecked) ---\n| Metric                  | Value         |\n|-------------------------|---------------|\n| Grad Norm: Recurrent Core | X.XXXXe-XX    |\n| Grad Norm: Final CNN      | Y.YYYYe-YY    |\n| Grad Norm: Shared Head    | Z.ZZZZe-ZZ    |\n| Ratio: CNN / Recurrent    | A.AAAA        |\n| Ratio: Recurrent / Head   | B.BBBB        |\n\n## --- Model B: d=6 / w=96 (High-Performing) ---\n| Metric\
    \                  | Value         |\n|-------------------------|---------------|\n| Grad Norm: Recurrent Core | P.PPPPe-PP    |\n| Grad Norm: Final CNN      | Q.QQQQe-QQ    |\n| Grad Norm: Shared Head    | R.RRRRe-RR    |\n| Ratio: CNN / Recurrent    | C.CCCC        |\n| Ratio: Recurrent / Head   | D.DDDD        |\n\n## --- Interpretation ---\n- [Auto-generated interpretation statement based on the results]\n```\n\n**Interpretation Logic:**\nThe `test()` function will conclude with a series of comparisons to automatically interpret the findings against my hypothesis.\n\n```python\n# In the test() function, after computing all metrics...\n\nprint(\"\\n## --- Interpretation ---\")\n\n# Compare Absolute Recurrent Gradients (Hypothesis 1)\nrecurrent_norm_a = ...\nrecurrent_norm_b = ...\nif recurrent_norm_a < recurrent_norm_b * 0.1: # Check for an order of magnitude difference\n    print(\"- CONFIRMED: Bottlenecked model (A) shows a drastically smaller recurrent gradient norm, suggesting\
    \ a vanishing gradient problem.\")\nelse:\n    print(\"- UNCONFIRMED: Absolute recurrent gradient norms are comparable.\")\n\n# Compare CNN-to-Recurrent Ratio (Hypothesis 2)\nratio_cr_a = ...\nratio_cr_b = ...\nif ratio_cr_a > ratio_cr_b * 5.0: # Check for a large imbalance\n    print(\"- CONFIRMED: Bottlenecked model (A) has a much higher CNN-to-Recurrent gradient ratio, suggesting the recurrent core is being starved of learning signal.\")\nelse:\n    print(\"- UNCONFIRMED: CNN-to-Recurrent gradient ratios are comparable.\")\n\n# Compare Recurrent-to-Head Ratio (Supporting evidence)\nratio_rh_a = ...\nratio_rh_b = ...\nif ratio_rh_a < ratio_rh_b * 0.5: # Check for a more severe drop-off\n    print(\"- SUPPORTED: Bottlenecked model (A) shows a more severe gradient decay from the head to the recurrent core.\")\nelse:\n    print(\"- UNSUPPORTED: Gradient decay from head to recurrent core is comparable.\")\n    \nreturn \"Gradient probe complete. See report above.\"\n```\n\nThis structure\
    \ ensures that the experiment's output is not just a collection of numbers, but a direct verdict on the hypotheses I set out to test. The thresholds for confirmation (e.g., `* 0.1`, `* 5.0`) are chosen to be conservative, requiring a strong signal to avoid spurious conclusions from minor numerical differences. This design makes the probe's result unambiguous and immediately actionable for my next research steps."
