    \ implement my procedure correctly. My next submission will *only* print the structure of the gradient PyTree. It will not attempt any analysis. This will give me the ground truth I need. Once I have the correct path, and only then, will I submit the full probe.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Kairos I - Research Protocol v2.0**\n\n  **Protocol:**\n  1.  **Literature Review:** Before hypothesizing, ensure full awareness of the existing SOTA in the Archive Room.\n  2.  **Hypothesize:** Formulate a clear, falsifiable hypothesis that builds upon, and explicitly references, prior art.\n  3.  **Implement & Test:** Build upon trusted, replicated baselines. Isolate variables meticulously.\n  4.  **Document & Analyze:** Maintain a rigorous log of all findings, successful or not.\n\n  **Current TODO:**\n  - **[IN PROGRESS]** Debugging Gradient Probe. Multiple `KeyError` failures. Now implementing a \"PyTree-printing\" submission to get the exact parameter paths before attempting\
    \ analysis again.\n  - **[NEXT]** Use the output of the PyTree probe to write the final, correct version of the Gradient Probe and submit it.\n```\n\n/execute_action{reply 7}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: Gradient Probe Failure v3 (ID 526) & Final Debugging Strategy\"\ncontent: |\n  My third attempt at the Gradient Probe has failed with the same type of error. My assumptions about Flax's internal naming conventions have been consistently wrong, and my attempts to guess the correct paths have been a waste of resources.\n\n  My process must become more rigorous. I am now abandoning any attempt at analysis until I have the ground truth.\n\n  My next submission (ID 526) will be a diagnostic tool only. It will:\n  1.  Calculate the gradients as before.\n  2.  Print the entire structure of the resulting PyTree of gradients.\n  3.  Perform no analysis.\n\n  This will provide the exact, undeniable paths to the parameters I need. I will then use this information to build the final, correct version of\
    \ the probe. There will be no more guesswork.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Gradient Probe v4 (Debug: Print PyTree)\"\ntags: \"mechanistic probe, test-only, debug, pytree\"\nabstract: \"A diagnostic, non-analytical submission designed to resolve a recurring KeyError in my Gradient Probe. This `test()` function will compute the gradients for my target architecture and print the complete structure of the resulting PyTree. This will provide the exact, correct paths to the layer parameters needed for the final version of the probe.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  \n  # Using the same architecture definition as the previous failed attempt\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      dilation: int\n      ratio: float = 0.25\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray) -> jnp.ndarray:\n          y =\
    \ nn.Conv(features=int(self.features * self.ratio), kernel_size=(1, 1))(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=int(self.features * self.ratio), kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation))(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1, 1))(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class GradientProbeSotaNet(nn.Module):\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      dilation: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, Dict]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          spatial_features, time_feature_map = x[..., :7], x[..., 7]\n          time_scalar = time_feature_map[:, 0, 0].reshape(-1, 1)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=32, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME', name=\"CNN_Block_1\")(spatial_features)\n          y = BottleneckBlock(features=32, dilation=self.dilation, name=\"CNN_Block_2\"\
    )(y)\n          y = BottleneckBlock(features=32, dilation=self.dilation, name=\"CNN_Block_3\")(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=64, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME', name=\"CNN_Block_4\")(y)\n          y = BottleneckBlock(features=64, dilation=self.dilation, name=\"CNN_Block_5\")(y)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(features=64, dilation=self.dilation, name=\"CNN_Final_Block\")(y)\n          conv_lstm_cell = nn.ConvLSTMCell(features=self.conv_lstm_features, kernel_size=(3, 3), name=\"recurrent_core\")\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              h, w = cnn_out.shape[1], cnn_out.shape[2]\n              dummy_c, dummy_h = jnp.zeros((batch_size, h, w, self.conv_lstm_features)), jnp.zeros((batch_size, h, w, self.conv_lstm_features))\n              rnn_state = (dummy_c, dummy_h)\n          done_mask_spatial = done.reshape(batch_size, 1, 1, 1)\n          rnn_state = jax.tree.map(lambda val: val * (1 - done_mask_spatial), rnn_state)\n          rnn_state, y = conv_lstm_cell(rnn_state,\
    \ cnn_out)\n          rnn_state, y = conv_lstm_cell(rnn_state, cnn_out)\n          h, w, c = y.shape[1], y.shape[2], y.shape[3]\n          y_flat = y.reshape(batch_size, h * w, c)\n          attn_logits = nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"attention_logits\")(y_flat).squeeze(-1)\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=-1)\n          attn_pooled = jnp.sum(y_flat * attn_weights[..., None], axis=1)\n          gap_pooled = jnp.mean(y_flat, axis=1)\n          pooled_features = jnp.concatenate([attn_pooled, gap_pooled], axis=-1)\n          final_features = jnp.concatenate([pooled_features, time_scalar], axis=-1)\n          x_head = nn.Dense(features=256, name=\"shared_head\")(final_features)\n          x_head = nn.relu(x_head)\n          policy_logits, value = nn.Dense(features=4, name=\"policy_head\")(x_head), nn.Dense(features=1, name=\"value_head\")(x_head).squeeze(-1)\n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def test():\n      rng = jax.random.PRNGKey(42)\n      dummy_obs,\
    \ dummy_done = jnp.zeros((32, 8, 8, 8)), jnp.zeros((32,), dtype=jnp.bool_)\n      fixed_input = jax.random.normal(rng, (32, 8, 8, 8))\n      \n      model = GradientProbeSotaNet(dilation=7, conv_lstm_features=64)\n      params = model.init(rng, dummy_obs, dummy_done, None)['params']\n      \n      def loss_fn(p):\n          _, value, _ = model.apply({'params': p}, fixed_input, dummy_done, None)\n          return jnp.mean(value)\n      \n      grads = jax.grad(loss_fn)(params)\n      \n      # The sole purpose of this run is to get the structure.\n      print(\"--- Gradient PyTree Structure ---\")\n      # Use jax.tree_util.tree_map to print shapes, which is more concise than printing raw arrays.\n      grad_shapes = jax.tree_util.tree_map(lambda x: x.shape, grads)\n      print(grad_shapes)\n      print(\"---------------------------------\")\n      \n      return \"PyTree structure printed to STDOUT. Check logs for the exact parameter paths.\"\n```"
